WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.480 --> 00:00:04.530
- Okay, hi, everybody, welcome.

00:00:04.530 --> 00:00:05.943
We're gonna get started.

00:00:07.735 --> 00:00:10.120
My name is Daniel Martinez HoSang.

00:00:10.120 --> 00:00:13.581
I'm a faculty member in
Ethnicity, Race, and Migration.

00:00:13.581 --> 00:00:15.330
I can't just tell you how amazing it is

00:00:15.330 --> 00:00:20.127
to see on a brutally cold afternoon,

00:00:21.457 --> 00:00:26.220
how great it is, and the
warmth that we sense in here,

00:00:26.220 --> 00:00:28.163
for this really, really
important conversation.

00:00:29.576 --> 00:00:31.680
I'm gonna just do, and I'm gonna moderate,

00:00:31.680 --> 00:00:33.916
I'll introduce our speakers in a moment,

00:00:33.916 --> 00:00:37.277
but just start by a
couple of the, you know,

00:00:37.277 --> 00:00:38.641
there's a lot, a lot of work

00:00:38.641 --> 00:00:41.490
that went into pulling this
off in just a couple of days.

00:00:41.490 --> 00:00:43.890
So, we first wanna thank our colleagues

00:00:43.890 --> 00:00:46.960
in the Yale Education Studies Program,

00:00:46.960 --> 00:00:48.933
and especially the Director Mira Debs,

00:00:49.877 --> 00:00:51.302
who just worked tirelessly.

00:00:51.302 --> 00:00:54.469
(audience applauding)

00:00:57.270 --> 00:00:59.995
I'll introduce in a moment

00:00:59.995 --> 00:01:02.639
our colleague Paul Van
Tassel from the Senate.

00:01:02.639 --> 00:01:04.350
But a lot of this, I mean,

00:01:04.350 --> 00:01:06.859
this event is sponsored principally

00:01:06.859 --> 00:01:08.380
by the Yale Faculty Senate,

00:01:08.380 --> 00:01:10.081
and it means an enormous amount

00:01:10.081 --> 00:01:12.150
that our colleagues across
many, many departments

00:01:12.150 --> 00:01:15.220
and programs and schools
understand that when we're saying

00:01:16.177 --> 00:01:17.520
there's something very,
very serious happening

00:01:17.520 --> 00:01:19.290
in our discipline and in our world,

00:01:19.290 --> 00:01:21.000
that they respond to that call,

00:01:21.000 --> 00:01:22.170
and wanna know more about it,

00:01:22.170 --> 00:01:24.541
and wanna know how they can be engaged.

00:01:24.541 --> 00:01:25.770
So we're grateful to
the Yale Faculty Senate,

00:01:25.770 --> 00:01:27.699
to the Gilder Lehrman Center,

00:01:27.699 --> 00:01:30.960
the Anti-Racist Teaching
and Learning Collective,

00:01:30.960 --> 00:01:34.050
and the many teachers who
are involved with that.

00:01:34.050 --> 00:01:37.530
African American Studies,
my colleague Jason Stanley,

00:01:37.530 --> 00:01:40.621
and of course, the African
American Policy Forum.

00:01:40.621 --> 00:01:42.304
So, just first, please join me

00:01:42.304 --> 00:01:43.720
in thanking all of our co-sponsors.

00:01:43.720 --> 00:01:46.887
(audience applauding)

00:01:50.501 --> 00:01:51.778
I'll also just say, you know,

00:01:51.778 --> 00:01:54.181
our aspiration here is
to listen to one another,

00:01:54.181 --> 00:01:55.223
to learn from one another.

00:01:56.280 --> 00:01:59.490
I imagine anyone that had any shady plans

00:01:59.490 --> 00:02:03.217
to disrupt this event would
find it too cold to be outside

00:02:03.217 --> 00:02:04.462
(audience laughing)

00:02:04.462 --> 00:02:05.542
and then make their way here.

00:02:05.542 --> 00:02:08.673
But we're looking forward
to generative exchanges

00:02:09.930 --> 00:02:13.323
that can help us all become
sharper thinkers and actors.

00:02:14.199 --> 00:02:16.020
Okay, so with that, I wanna
invite my colleague, Paul,

00:02:16.020 --> 00:02:18.259
to come up and just say a few words

00:02:18.259 --> 00:02:19.981
on behalf of the Yale Faculty Senate,

00:02:19.981 --> 00:02:21.449
and then we'll get into our panel.

00:02:21.449 --> 00:02:22.550
Thank you.

00:02:22.550 --> 00:02:26.271
(audience applauding)

00:02:26.271 --> 00:02:27.180
- Thank you, Professor HoSang.

00:02:27.180 --> 00:02:29.760
It's just, I'm delighted to be here today

00:02:29.760 --> 00:02:31.920
to lead the Senate this year,

00:02:31.920 --> 00:02:34.230
and to help sponsor such an event.

00:02:34.230 --> 00:02:37.290
The Senate stands fully behind

00:02:37.290 --> 00:02:40.950
promoting these types of events
where we engage in dialogue,

00:02:40.950 --> 00:02:42.572
we listen to one another,

00:02:42.572 --> 00:02:43.405
we learn from one another,

00:02:43.405 --> 00:02:45.720
and especially when the room is so packed

00:02:45.720 --> 00:02:47.703
that people can hardly sit.

00:02:48.870 --> 00:02:50.670
So, again, I'm Paul Van Tassel.

00:02:50.670 --> 00:02:54.983
I am a professor in Chemical
and Environmental Engineering

00:02:54.983 --> 00:02:55.816
here at Yale.

00:02:55.816 --> 00:02:57.960
This year I happen to be
chair of the Faculty Senate.

00:02:57.960 --> 00:03:00.167
Maybe many of you are students,

00:03:00.167 --> 00:03:01.000
you might not know we
have a Faculty Senate.

00:03:01.000 --> 00:03:01.980
We do have a Faculty Senate.

00:03:01.980 --> 00:03:04.788
It's a little bit like
your Yale College Council

00:03:04.788 --> 00:03:05.621
or the Graduate Student Assembly.

00:03:05.621 --> 00:03:08.460
It's the principal governing
structure of faculty

00:03:08.460 --> 00:03:10.560
here at Yale University.

00:03:10.560 --> 00:03:12.930
And we're excited about
a lot of our activities,

00:03:12.930 --> 00:03:16.443
among them sponsoring
wonderful events like this.

00:03:17.353 --> 00:03:22.353
And principle, maybe as
the single unifying topic

00:03:22.590 --> 00:03:24.030
that we address as a senate

00:03:24.030 --> 00:03:26.310
is this concept of academic freedom.

00:03:26.310 --> 00:03:28.620
I think everybody knows,
I know students are here

00:03:28.620 --> 00:03:30.090
from many different levels,

00:03:30.090 --> 00:03:34.439
but academic freedom is
basically, as it sounds, right?

00:03:34.439 --> 00:03:35.820
It's the freedom as a professor

00:03:35.820 --> 00:03:38.070
to teach what we feel should be taught,

00:03:38.070 --> 00:03:40.890
to research what we feel
should be researched

00:03:40.890 --> 00:03:43.590
and engage in dialogue freely

00:03:43.590 --> 00:03:47.400
and in debate freely about
what we feel is important.

00:03:47.400 --> 00:03:50.902
The same principles hold for students too,

00:03:50.902 --> 00:03:52.530
and other educators.

00:03:52.530 --> 00:03:56.970
We feel, for me perhaps,
it's my most cherished aspect

00:03:56.970 --> 00:04:01.200
of my job, is that I have the
freedom to do those things.

00:04:01.200 --> 00:04:04.320
And today's topic strikes
really at the heart

00:04:04.320 --> 00:04:06.210
of academic freedom.

00:04:06.210 --> 00:04:09.570
The Senate among its other activities

00:04:09.570 --> 00:04:11.939
recently has released a resolution

00:04:11.939 --> 00:04:16.839
speaking against these so-called
educational gag orders.

00:04:16.839 --> 00:04:20.920
It's a phenomena that goes
beyond just the discussion

00:04:20.920 --> 00:04:21.753
that we'll have today,

00:04:21.753 --> 00:04:24.501
but it encompasses the
discussion we'll have today.

00:04:24.501 --> 00:04:26.160
And these have to do with legislations

00:04:26.160 --> 00:04:29.400
that might act to
restrict academic freedoms

00:04:29.400 --> 00:04:31.680
on various levels.

00:04:31.680 --> 00:04:33.240
And so, again, we're very, very happy

00:04:33.240 --> 00:04:36.717
to be able to sponsor
this important event.

00:04:36.717 --> 00:04:40.050
And I should point out
too, that today's teaching,

00:04:40.050 --> 00:04:41.190
no matter who was presenting,

00:04:41.190 --> 00:04:43.437
this would be a very important topic

00:04:43.437 --> 00:04:45.720
and one certainly worthy
of a nice discussion.

00:04:45.720 --> 00:04:48.750
But in fact, in this case,
we have two individuals

00:04:48.750 --> 00:04:52.110
that have been targeted
by such legislation.

00:04:52.110 --> 00:04:55.200
So my own Yale colleague,
Roderick Ferguson,

00:04:55.200 --> 00:04:58.479
and colleague of sorts, but from Columbia,

00:04:58.479 --> 00:05:00.720
Kimberlé Crenshaw, who
themselves have been targeted,

00:05:00.720 --> 00:05:04.737
and their academic freedom
has been put in jeopardy

00:05:04.737 --> 00:05:09.706
at least potentially by
these types of legislation.

00:05:09.706 --> 00:05:10.800
So with that,

00:05:10.800 --> 00:05:12.087
again welcome everybody,

00:05:12.087 --> 00:05:14.163
and I look forward to a great discussion.

00:05:15.116 --> 00:05:18.283
(audience applauding)

00:05:22.755 --> 00:05:23.774
- All right.

00:05:23.774 --> 00:05:24.775
So I'm gonna do,

00:05:24.775 --> 00:05:25.830
and I should also mention our colleague

00:05:25.830 --> 00:05:27.510
Daisha Brabham, who is a member

00:05:27.510 --> 00:05:29.700
of the anti-racist Teaching
and Learning Collective,

00:05:29.700 --> 00:05:32.550
a brilliant teacher at
Windsor High School,

00:05:32.550 --> 00:05:33.690
was not able to come today,

00:05:33.690 --> 00:05:36.120
I suspect she's watching
on the livestream.

00:05:36.120 --> 00:05:37.023
So, hi Daisha.

00:05:38.138 --> 00:05:39.630
I'm gonna do my best to
incorporate some of the comments

00:05:39.630 --> 00:05:41.640
that Daisha shared with us in advance.

00:05:41.640 --> 00:05:44.060
I'm gonna say something very quick

00:05:44.060 --> 00:05:46.140
to try to kind of get us on
the same page as best I can

00:05:46.140 --> 00:05:48.120
about what's happened in
the last couple of weeks.

00:05:48.120 --> 00:05:51.360
And then we're gonna do a
series of kind of rounds

00:05:51.360 --> 00:05:52.620
to get each of the panelists

00:05:52.620 --> 00:05:54.957
in conversation with one another.

00:05:54.957 --> 00:05:57.030
What are they seeing, hearing, noticing?

00:05:57.030 --> 00:05:58.473
What are the opportunities?

00:05:59.359 --> 00:06:00.192
Throughout, there'll be a chance

00:06:00.192 --> 00:06:03.030
for you to share your
questions for the panelists.

00:06:03.030 --> 00:06:06.525
And the brilliant APF staff will help

00:06:06.525 --> 00:06:09.044
to kind of curate them so that
we can engage your questions

00:06:09.044 --> 00:06:10.101
at the end.

00:06:10.101 --> 00:06:11.880
And then I think at 5:30,
we'll all be going upstairs

00:06:11.880 --> 00:06:14.630
for the reception afterwards
and have a chance to talk.

00:06:15.703 --> 00:06:18.267
Okay, so over the last 10
years, the College Board,

00:06:21.420 --> 00:06:23.946
which is formally a nonprofit,

00:06:23.946 --> 00:06:25.710
but is responsible for administering

00:06:25.710 --> 00:06:26.910
all the standardized tests

00:06:26.910 --> 00:06:30.120
that many students were
subjected to in this room,

00:06:30.120 --> 00:06:33.099
as well as AP courses,
Advanced Placement courses,

00:06:33.099 --> 00:06:36.750
which of course, are intended
to resemble college courses

00:06:36.750 --> 00:06:38.760
and in many cases offer college credit

00:06:38.760 --> 00:06:41.400
to develop a course in
African American studies.

00:06:41.400 --> 00:06:44.490
The very fact that after
many, many decades,

00:06:44.490 --> 00:06:46.500
the AP first decided

00:06:46.500 --> 00:06:49.020
that it would be finally
appropriate to do this

00:06:49.020 --> 00:06:52.560
is in effect of many,
many students and faculty

00:06:52.560 --> 00:06:53.850
and scholars and advocates

00:06:53.850 --> 00:06:58.850
demanding the inclusion of
these bodies of knowledge.

00:06:59.040 --> 00:07:02.220
About two weeks ago, maybe a little more,

00:07:02.220 --> 00:07:05.242
the governor of Florida, Ron DeSantis

00:07:05.242 --> 00:07:06.300
and the Education Department announced,

00:07:06.300 --> 00:07:08.460
there had been previous drafts circulated,

00:07:08.460 --> 00:07:12.600
all AP courses are often
done in close collaboration

00:07:12.600 --> 00:07:15.645
with college faculty who
are experts in their field

00:07:15.645 --> 00:07:16.478
because they're intended to replicate

00:07:16.478 --> 00:07:18.240
what a college course would be,

00:07:18.240 --> 00:07:22.710
announced that the course
had no educational value

00:07:22.710 --> 00:07:24.033
for Florida students.

00:07:25.084 --> 00:07:27.210
And a day or two later released a graphic

00:07:27.210 --> 00:07:30.300
trying to identify bodies of both scholars

00:07:30.300 --> 00:07:31.650
and bodies of knowledge

00:07:31.650 --> 00:07:34.320
that it said would not be taught

00:07:34.320 --> 00:07:36.300
to Florida high school students

00:07:36.300 --> 00:07:39.870
until the College Board revised that.

00:07:39.870 --> 00:07:42.420
Among the scholars listed
were our colleagues

00:07:42.420 --> 00:07:45.810
Professor Crenshaw, Professor
Ferguson, Bell Hooks,

00:07:45.810 --> 00:07:47.160
Angela Davis.

00:07:47.160 --> 00:07:49.230
Bodies of knowledge
including queer studies,

00:07:49.230 --> 00:07:52.410
social movements, abolition, et cetera.

00:07:52.410 --> 00:07:56.640
By some coincidence, three days
later, as the story unfolds,

00:07:56.640 --> 00:08:00.390
the College Board announced
that in its revised curriculum,

00:08:00.390 --> 00:08:03.640
all of those thinkers and bodies
of knowledge and many more

00:08:04.624 --> 00:08:06.360
had now been moved to an optional section.

00:08:06.360 --> 00:08:07.620
So they were effectively,

00:08:07.620 --> 00:08:12.180
effectively students could
study them as essay topics

00:08:12.180 --> 00:08:14.310
and for further discussion

00:08:14.310 --> 00:08:17.340
and disavow that it was in any way related

00:08:17.340 --> 00:08:18.930
to the earlier round of criticism.

00:08:18.930 --> 00:08:21.780
And we'll talk about some
of the reasons they cited.

00:08:21.780 --> 00:08:23.640
So here we are now today.

00:08:23.640 --> 00:08:26.160
The College Board effectively signing off

00:08:26.160 --> 00:08:29.820
on a kind of hiving off and
sanitizing of the curriculum

00:08:29.820 --> 00:08:31.710
and thinking about what was at stake,

00:08:31.710 --> 00:08:34.200
who was named in those exclusions,

00:08:34.200 --> 00:08:36.930
and what the loss is and
how that reverberates.

00:08:36.930 --> 00:08:40.563
So for the first round of
questions, I want to, hi, Kim.

00:08:41.397 --> 00:08:42.240
- Hi.

00:08:42.240 --> 00:08:43.650
- Welcome.

00:08:43.650 --> 00:08:48.450
So, and Kim and the APF, of
course, have been crucial

00:08:48.450 --> 00:08:51.870
since the attacks on
critical race theory began

00:08:51.870 --> 00:08:54.178
three years ago now, I think.

00:08:54.178 --> 00:08:55.800
That's right, in helping
us think about the link

00:08:55.800 --> 00:08:58.830
between what's happening in
Florida, what a resolution

00:08:58.830 --> 00:09:01.140
that we may be paying
attention to in Texas,

00:09:01.140 --> 00:09:03.780
and kind of a broad set of concerns.

00:09:03.780 --> 00:09:06.540
So I wanna ask each of you, and I'll just,

00:09:06.540 --> 00:09:08.653
I'm gonna do these super quick.

00:09:08.653 --> 00:09:09.486
Our colleague David Blight in history,

00:09:09.486 --> 00:09:11.010
Jason Stanley in philosophy,

00:09:11.010 --> 00:09:14.349
and Roderick Ferguson in
women and Gender Studies,

00:09:14.349 --> 00:09:16.816
ERM and African American Studies.

00:09:16.816 --> 00:09:18.840
It has been a whirlwind, just a whirlwind

00:09:18.840 --> 00:09:21.840
from the time we started
planning this, right,

00:09:21.840 --> 00:09:23.673
two other chapters have unfolded.

00:09:24.854 --> 00:09:26.150
So I just wanna first start,

00:09:26.150 --> 00:09:28.080
we're gonna get into the
analysis, the unpacking,

00:09:28.080 --> 00:09:30.840
but I wanna get a sense of
what you've seen, right?

00:09:30.840 --> 00:09:35.840
What, across all of this,
the headlines, the claims,

00:09:35.955 --> 00:09:37.680
the indignities, what stood out to you,

00:09:37.680 --> 00:09:40.533
and maybe just share a moment or, yeah,

00:09:42.120 --> 00:09:44.490
something you read that
really kind of captures it.

00:09:44.490 --> 00:09:46.200
So that'll be our first round,
and then we'll go from there.

00:09:46.200 --> 00:09:48.639
And Kim, I'd love to start with you.

00:09:48.639 --> 00:09:50.080
- All right.

00:09:50.080 --> 00:09:52.050
Well, good afternoon, everyone.

00:09:52.050 --> 00:09:54.856
When I heard about the weather,

00:09:54.856 --> 00:09:58.593
I thought there'd be five people
here, so I am overwhelmed.

00:09:59.761 --> 00:10:01.080
So, great to be here.

00:10:01.080 --> 00:10:04.770
So I wanna talk about the headlines.

00:10:04.770 --> 00:10:08.430
Because those are, those
are the things that

00:10:08.430 --> 00:10:13.430
in my mind reinforce one of the problems

00:10:13.470 --> 00:10:16.236
that we've been seeing for two years.

00:10:16.236 --> 00:10:18.690
And that is the way the
media have functioned

00:10:18.690 --> 00:10:20.996
somewhat as a third arm

00:10:20.996 --> 00:10:25.470
in this effort to
politicize the university,

00:10:25.470 --> 00:10:28.380
to demonize critical race theory,

00:10:28.380 --> 00:10:31.200
intersectionality in particular,

00:10:31.200 --> 00:10:36.200
and to create what in its best
moments is a both sides-ism,

00:10:36.540 --> 00:10:39.690
but sometimes it doesn't
even arise to that level.

00:10:39.690 --> 00:10:43.593
So here's one of the headlines
from the New York Times.

00:10:45.261 --> 00:10:49.087
"DeSantis Takes on the
Education Establishment

00:10:49.087 --> 00:10:51.627
"and Builds his Brand."

00:10:52.860 --> 00:10:56.331
So let's just ponder
this for a moment, right?

00:10:56.331 --> 00:10:59.733
This is, basically this could
come from the sports page,

00:11:00.732 --> 00:11:05.280
you know, a general manager
of X and X team, you know,

00:11:05.280 --> 00:11:06.990
acquires such and such,

00:11:06.990 --> 00:11:10.135
and they're in line for the Super Bowl.

00:11:10.135 --> 00:11:10.968
It's sort of treating

00:11:10.968 --> 00:11:15.750
some of the most significant
challenges to our democracy

00:11:15.750 --> 00:11:19.530
as a question of
branding, as a question of

00:11:19.530 --> 00:11:21.630
is this going to be the strategy

00:11:21.630 --> 00:11:23.820
that puts him into the White House?

00:11:23.820 --> 00:11:27.030
You don't get a sense in this headline

00:11:27.030 --> 00:11:28.410
that what we're looking at

00:11:28.410 --> 00:11:31.410
is the attack on public education,

00:11:31.410 --> 00:11:34.830
the attack on more than
70 years of building

00:11:34.830 --> 00:11:36.780
a civil rights infrastructure

00:11:36.780 --> 00:11:40.590
that's backed up by and
amplified by the kind of work

00:11:40.590 --> 00:11:43.080
that people who are deeply invested

00:11:43.080 --> 00:11:45.390
in addressing the history of this country

00:11:45.390 --> 00:11:48.960
and its contemporary implications
for our current democracy.

00:11:48.960 --> 00:11:52.801
You don't get a sense that
any of that is in there.

00:11:52.801 --> 00:11:55.590
So for me, this is an illustration

00:11:55.590 --> 00:11:57.780
of how what we're facing

00:11:57.780 --> 00:12:00.510
is not just a coordinated, organized,

00:12:00.510 --> 00:12:03.870
and well-funded campaign
on the part of those

00:12:03.870 --> 00:12:07.590
who seek to make racism erasable,

00:12:07.590 --> 00:12:10.195
those who seek to make Black history

00:12:10.195 --> 00:12:13.230
basically end at 1960.

00:12:13.230 --> 00:12:16.140
We're also fighting against a media

00:12:16.140 --> 00:12:19.140
that's taken a kind of,
well, maybe/maybe not

00:12:19.140 --> 00:12:20.010
kind of approach.

00:12:20.010 --> 00:12:22.170
And now with the presidential campaign,

00:12:22.170 --> 00:12:25.800
it's turning into more of the
sort of sports commentary.

00:12:25.800 --> 00:12:27.510
I suggest that we think about

00:12:27.510 --> 00:12:30.960
what some alternatives
might have been to that.

00:12:30.960 --> 00:12:35.960
Maybe, you know, the war
against academic freedom

00:12:37.056 --> 00:12:40.519
takes a victory, right?

00:12:40.519 --> 00:12:42.990
The war is against us,

00:12:42.990 --> 00:12:47.990
has gotten an important
victory under its belt.

00:12:48.199 --> 00:12:52.350
Or Black studies is the next venue

00:12:52.350 --> 00:12:55.320
in the war against academic freedom.

00:12:55.320 --> 00:12:58.230
That would give you a
little bit more of a sense

00:12:58.230 --> 00:13:00.337
of what is going on,

00:13:00.337 --> 00:13:04.615
or maybe College Board and DeSantis align

00:13:04.615 --> 00:13:06.393
in their blow to academic freedom.

00:13:07.320 --> 00:13:08.670
Right, does that get
us a little bit closer?

00:13:08.670 --> 00:13:09.840
Or how about this?

00:13:09.840 --> 00:13:13.860
DeSantis unveils his
racially charged strategy

00:13:13.860 --> 00:13:15.273
to the White House?

00:13:16.515 --> 00:13:17.400
Maybe that gets us a little bit closer,

00:13:17.400 --> 00:13:19.680
but I would say it just reinforced

00:13:19.680 --> 00:13:22.740
how much the media is also
a site of contestation

00:13:22.740 --> 00:13:23.583
in this moment.

00:13:24.439 --> 00:13:28.758
- Yeah, isolated unfolding of events.

00:13:28.758 --> 00:13:30.450
That's just a kind of
campaign curiosity, spectacle,

00:13:30.450 --> 00:13:31.283
- Yes.

00:13:32.154 --> 00:13:32.987
- To do that, right?

00:13:32.987 --> 00:13:33.937
Okay, Rod, let's turn to you.

00:13:33.937 --> 00:13:36.390
You know, I imagine you're,

00:13:36.390 --> 00:13:39.210
I had just this thought
you were, I don't know,

00:13:39.210 --> 00:13:41.355
getting ready to like, yeah,

00:13:41.355 --> 00:13:43.353
I think you could just turn it, yeah.

00:13:44.974 --> 00:13:47.553
- Jason wants his microphone.

00:13:50.255 --> 00:13:52.620
You might have turned it off and on.

00:13:52.620 --> 00:13:53.453
Can you hear me?

00:13:53.453 --> 00:13:54.948
- Yeah, yeah.

00:13:54.948 --> 00:13:55.954
- All right, good.

00:13:55.954 --> 00:13:57.750
- Yeah, yes, so, Rod, you were
thrust in the middle of this.

00:13:57.750 --> 00:14:00.150
I imagine like without much notice.

00:14:00.150 --> 00:14:02.210
You wrote a brilliant piece

00:14:02.210 --> 00:14:03.630
in the Chronicle of Higher Education,

00:14:03.630 --> 00:14:05.610
which everyone should read.

00:14:05.610 --> 00:14:06.760
What stands out to you?

00:14:08.191 --> 00:14:10.673
- You know, first of all,
it's great to see everybody.

00:14:10.673 --> 00:14:14.400
I see some of mine in the
room, former students,

00:14:14.400 --> 00:14:17.667
so great to see all of
you and also colleagues.

00:14:17.667 --> 00:14:20.730
I think that for me, it, you know,

00:14:20.730 --> 00:14:24.300
the headlines that suggested
the sort of degrading

00:14:24.300 --> 00:14:27.573
of critical thinking and
critical pedagogy, you know,

00:14:28.446 --> 00:14:30.390
those were the things that stuck out.

00:14:30.390 --> 00:14:32.274
So one headline is

00:14:32.274 --> 00:14:35.692
"The College Board strips
down it's AP Curriculum

00:14:35.692 --> 00:14:37.530
for African American Studies."

00:14:37.530 --> 00:14:39.180
The official course looks
different from a previous draft,

00:14:39.180 --> 00:14:41.040
no more critical race theory,

00:14:41.040 --> 00:14:43.470
and the study of contemporary topics

00:14:43.470 --> 00:14:46.766
like Black Lives Matter is optional.

00:14:46.766 --> 00:14:48.472
So I used to teach writing, right?

00:14:48.472 --> 00:14:49.305
That's how I cut my teeth.

00:14:50.393 --> 00:14:52.230
And what's constructed,

00:14:52.230 --> 00:14:54.480
what's implied in this headline

00:14:54.480 --> 00:14:57.840
is that critical race
theory, Black Lives Matter,

00:14:57.840 --> 00:14:59.460
all of the things that we associated

00:14:59.460 --> 00:15:03.000
with contemporary struggles are excess.

00:15:03.000 --> 00:15:04.560
You know, they don't belong.

00:15:04.560 --> 00:15:05.580
They're not needed.

00:15:05.580 --> 00:15:07.333
They're unnecessary.

00:15:07.333 --> 00:15:10.893
And that we can do away with them, right?

00:15:12.390 --> 00:15:15.210
At that moment, you
know, we have an assault,

00:15:15.210 --> 00:15:17.850
not just on critical race theory.

00:15:17.850 --> 00:15:20.670
We also have an assault
on critical pedagogy,

00:15:20.670 --> 00:15:23.343
critical thinking, literacy.

00:15:24.608 --> 00:15:26.008
That should alarm all of us.

00:15:26.872 --> 00:15:29.113
You know, no matter
our disciplines, right?

00:15:29.113 --> 00:15:31.110
There's a way in which, you know,

00:15:31.110 --> 00:15:32.654
focusing on African American studies

00:15:32.654 --> 00:15:37.080
makes it seem as if it's a special object,

00:15:37.080 --> 00:15:39.190
a specialized target.

00:15:39.190 --> 00:15:41.520
But this is actually a
pretty general assault

00:15:41.520 --> 00:15:44.193
on all education and critical literacy.

00:15:45.053 --> 00:15:48.273
So those are the things that
stood out for me as a teacher.

00:15:49.560 --> 00:15:52.414
- Yeah, and there's such
an important move there

00:15:52.414 --> 00:15:54.390
to kind of reduce a
course to simply content,

00:15:54.390 --> 00:15:56.520
to simply mastering a body of content.

00:15:56.520 --> 00:16:00.660
That's not a, that's
not just a subtle shift

00:16:00.660 --> 00:16:02.520
in what's incorporated, right?

00:16:02.520 --> 00:16:04.470
That's announcing what a class is for

00:16:04.470 --> 00:16:06.410
and what it's not for.

00:16:06.410 --> 00:16:07.408
- Right.

00:16:07.408 --> 00:16:08.413
- And you sit there.

00:16:08.413 --> 00:16:10.410
All right, Jason, all the way on the end

00:16:10.410 --> 00:16:11.943
with your own microphone.

00:16:14.603 --> 00:16:15.606
What did you see?

00:16:15.606 --> 00:16:17.442
Yes, and I'll just say also,
Jason, you've, you know,

00:16:17.442 --> 00:16:18.647
written a lot, obviously
about (all laughing).

00:16:24.540 --> 00:16:25.373
Public goods.

00:16:27.492 --> 00:16:29.010
Okay, about, you know,
currents of fascism,

00:16:29.010 --> 00:16:32.989
which seems not another
time, another place,

00:16:32.989 --> 00:16:37.110
how they show up, how
they shape these events.

00:16:37.110 --> 00:16:38.703
So yeah, what stood out to you?

00:16:39.810 --> 00:16:41.553
- So, two things stood out.

00:16:42.705 --> 00:16:45.300
First, my work on fascism is based

00:16:45.300 --> 00:16:47.550
in the Black intellectual tradition.

00:16:47.550 --> 00:16:52.550
The way that theorists from
Langston Hughes to Angela Davis

00:16:52.860 --> 00:16:56.912
to Toni Morrison have talked
about fascist practices

00:16:56.912 --> 00:16:58.710
in the United States, like banning books,

00:16:58.710 --> 00:17:00.330
restricting education.

00:17:00.330 --> 00:17:03.570
And the person who has
taught me most about that

00:17:03.570 --> 00:17:06.960
is my colleague, Rod
Ferguson, who was declared,

00:17:06.960 --> 00:17:10.290
whose work was declared as
having no educational value.

00:17:10.290 --> 00:17:14.340
So that's deliberate, to
declare the sources of education

00:17:14.340 --> 00:17:16.440
as having no educational value.

00:17:16.440 --> 00:17:18.210
That's intentional.

00:17:18.210 --> 00:17:22.140
And the second thing
that really struck me,

00:17:22.140 --> 00:17:25.500
just in terms of the
paradoxicality of it all,

00:17:25.500 --> 00:17:27.420
was that Kimberlé Crenshaw

00:17:27.420 --> 00:17:30.420
was coming to get the Winslow
medal at my university,

00:17:30.420 --> 00:17:34.890
the highest medal given by
Yale School of Public Health

00:17:34.890 --> 00:17:37.920
for concepts such as intersectionality,

00:17:37.920 --> 00:17:42.920
which were being banned from
the curriculum in Florida

00:17:42.969 --> 00:17:45.150
as having no educational value.

00:17:45.150 --> 00:17:49.230
So no educational value means obviously

00:17:49.230 --> 00:17:52.368
very high educational value.

00:17:52.368 --> 00:17:54.810
(audience laughing)

00:17:54.810 --> 00:17:55.743
- Thanks, Jason.

00:17:58.387 --> 00:18:01.325
Professor Blight, you
know, a lot of your work

00:18:01.325 --> 00:18:03.870
gives us a historic context

00:18:03.870 --> 00:18:08.830
to understand moments
of revanchism, right?

00:18:08.830 --> 00:18:10.410
Reclaiming something that seems lost,

00:18:10.410 --> 00:18:11.883
from reconstruction,

00:18:12.900 --> 00:18:15.960
the kind of national reunion
quote unquote that followed.

00:18:15.960 --> 00:18:18.423
What did you see that caught your eye?

00:18:19.560 --> 00:18:20.569
- First of all,

00:18:20.569 --> 00:18:21.750
I've never used that fancy
word revanchism in print.

00:18:21.750 --> 00:18:23.484
- [Daniel] It's yours.

00:18:23.484 --> 00:18:24.960
- I need to think about that now.

00:18:24.960 --> 00:18:28.383
First of all, amazing turnout.

00:18:28.383 --> 00:18:29.490
Thank you all for, I see
a lot of my students,

00:18:29.490 --> 00:18:31.413
former students, incredible.

00:18:32.460 --> 00:18:34.781
Teach-ins are alive and well.

00:18:34.781 --> 00:18:35.614
How about that?

00:18:36.810 --> 00:18:39.813
Let me just describe what
I experienced this week.

00:18:40.920 --> 00:18:42.753
You know, in a little chronology.

00:18:43.606 --> 00:18:44.439
First of all, I agree the headlines

00:18:44.439 --> 00:18:46.100
could be one hell of a lot better.

00:18:47.250 --> 00:18:48.543
But here's what happened.

00:18:49.623 --> 00:18:52.868
A week ago today I got an
email from David Coleman,

00:18:52.868 --> 00:18:54.960
the head of the College Board
and two of his assistants

00:18:54.960 --> 00:18:58.888
asking me to endorse
the course, The Course.

00:18:58.888 --> 00:19:02.373
I said, well, fine, but
I'd like to read it first.

00:19:04.530 --> 00:19:08.280
His initial point was that I
should just have confidence

00:19:08.280 --> 00:19:10.410
in the people who are
the lead consultants.

00:19:10.410 --> 00:19:12.000
Now those are good friends of mine,

00:19:12.000 --> 00:19:14.750
but you still wanna read
something before you blurb it.

00:19:15.720 --> 00:19:19.020
The next day, they sent me the course.

00:19:19.020 --> 00:19:23.850
Turns out the course I
read is the final one

00:19:23.850 --> 00:19:26.938
that they were about to
release on Wednesday.

00:19:26.938 --> 00:19:30.735
The one with the excised
eight or 10 sections

00:19:30.735 --> 00:19:32.253
from the last part.

00:19:32.253 --> 00:19:33.086
I didn't know that.

00:19:34.644 --> 00:19:37.263
On Monday I wrote an endorsement,

00:19:39.457 --> 00:19:41.100
mostly about academic freedom.

00:19:41.100 --> 00:19:43.961
I just said the usual things
about academic freedom.

00:19:43.961 --> 00:19:46.297
I said, this is what this is about.

00:19:46.297 --> 00:19:49.050
And I said something like, no
state legislature, governor,

00:19:49.050 --> 00:19:54.050
or board of education has
any business messing in,

00:19:54.561 --> 00:19:55.618
I didn't use the word messing,

00:19:55.618 --> 00:19:59.867
but in course curriculum for
the public schools period.

00:20:01.545 --> 00:20:04.337
And then that afternoon,

00:20:04.337 --> 00:20:06.763
and let's give a little shout out here

00:20:06.763 --> 00:20:09.422
to some people in the journalism world

00:20:09.422 --> 00:20:11.425
who are actually trying to do their job.

00:20:11.425 --> 00:20:13.383
My own student, Rose, are you here?

00:20:14.257 --> 00:20:15.262
I don't know.

00:20:15.262 --> 00:20:16.561
I can't tell.

00:20:16.561 --> 00:20:18.978
She used to be editor of the Yale Daily.

00:20:18.978 --> 00:20:21.155
She's now working for NBC News.

00:20:21.155 --> 00:20:25.056
She gets me on the phone as
a person to be interviewed,

00:20:25.056 --> 00:20:27.353
not as her senior essay
advisor, which I also am.

00:20:30.000 --> 00:20:35.000
And Rose had the old version
that had been pirated out.

00:20:35.853 --> 00:20:37.260
And you could get it finally
through the Wall Street Journal

00:20:37.260 --> 00:20:38.433
and some other places.

00:20:39.300 --> 00:20:41.239
I had the final version.

00:20:41.239 --> 00:20:42.713
I didn't know there
was an earlier version.

00:20:45.140 --> 00:20:47.619
And so on the phone for more than an hour,

00:20:47.619 --> 00:20:49.470
Rose and I are comparing the two versions,

00:20:49.470 --> 00:20:51.822
point by point by point.

00:20:51.822 --> 00:20:55.263
And I thought, oh, uh
huh, this was all excised.

00:20:56.825 --> 00:20:59.329
So I that afternoon
withdrew my endorsement

00:20:59.329 --> 00:21:00.507
from Mr. Coleman.

00:21:00.507 --> 00:21:02.829
He respectfully said, thank you.

00:21:02.829 --> 00:21:04.830
I said, until I understand
what the hell happened here,

00:21:04.830 --> 00:21:06.480
no endorsement.

00:21:06.480 --> 00:21:08.630
Then I spent an hour
on the phone with Kim.

00:21:10.485 --> 00:21:12.090
I think that night, I can't remember now,

00:21:12.090 --> 00:21:15.031
just musing about what
the hell's going on.

00:21:15.031 --> 00:21:16.036
I don't know.

00:21:16.036 --> 00:21:16.869
What do you think's going on?

00:21:16.869 --> 00:21:19.106
What about this, what about that?

00:21:19.106 --> 00:21:20.110
We gossiped a lot

00:21:22.351 --> 00:21:24.473
about who actually is
consulting on this thing.

00:21:26.247 --> 00:21:29.400
And then one thing led
to another to another,

00:21:29.400 --> 00:21:31.860
all kinds, you know, you get
quoted in one press piece,

00:21:31.860 --> 00:21:33.930
and they all start calling you.

00:21:33.930 --> 00:21:38.930
Last point about that,
I think it's yesterday,

00:21:39.030 --> 00:21:42.240
I did an interview with a
guy from the Washington Post,

00:21:42.240 --> 00:21:45.049
Nick Anderson, I think his name is,

00:21:45.049 --> 00:21:46.043
he does higher ed for them.

00:21:47.092 --> 00:21:48.900
He had gone out and interviewed
a whole bunch of people

00:21:48.900 --> 00:21:52.503
who had worked on this
curriculum all through last year.

00:21:53.670 --> 00:21:56.103
Teachers, professors, et cetera.

00:21:57.013 --> 00:21:58.470
Some had just been asked to critique it.

00:21:58.470 --> 00:22:00.960
Others had actually conceived it.

00:22:00.960 --> 00:22:05.490
To a person, this was
information worth having,

00:22:05.490 --> 00:22:06.900
to a person they said,

00:22:06.900 --> 00:22:09.090
we were working on this all last year.

00:22:09.090 --> 00:22:11.949
We were doing all this
long before DeSantis

00:22:11.949 --> 00:22:13.151
ever said anything.

00:22:13.151 --> 00:22:14.162
We're not doing this.

00:22:14.162 --> 00:22:14.995
We didn't make changes.

00:22:14.995 --> 00:22:17.970
You need to know this,
in response to DeSantis.

00:22:17.970 --> 00:22:21.783
We made the changes for
substantive reasons.

00:22:21.783 --> 00:22:22.800
Now this is what we can still debate.

00:22:22.800 --> 00:22:24.480
What substantive reasons?

00:22:24.480 --> 00:22:26.790
Why only the stuff at
the end of the course?

00:22:26.790 --> 00:22:29.910
Why only the contemporary
stuff and so on and so forth.

00:22:29.910 --> 00:22:32.970
But it's not like they just heard,

00:22:32.970 --> 00:22:34.653
it's simply not true

00:22:34.653 --> 00:22:37.260
that they heard DeSantis's
news conference last week

00:22:37.260 --> 00:22:38.730
and suddenly made the changes.

00:22:38.730 --> 00:22:39.963
That's just not true.

00:22:40.800 --> 00:22:44.910
I also am keen to say,

00:22:44.910 --> 00:22:47.110
and when we come back
to other topics here,

00:22:47.110 --> 00:22:49.680
we're having this debate
on Ron DeSantis's terms,

00:22:49.680 --> 00:22:52.140
and we need to think otherwise.

00:22:52.140 --> 00:22:54.690
Now that's not easy because
they have millions of dollars

00:22:54.690 --> 00:22:56.670
behind this campaign
against public schools.

00:22:56.670 --> 00:22:59.220
They have Koch Brothers
money behind this campaign.

00:23:00.369 --> 00:23:02.400
The educational community,
academia, teachers,

00:23:02.400 --> 00:23:06.450
especially teachers
don't have big lobbies,

00:23:06.450 --> 00:23:08.733
and they don't have
big propaganda budgets.

00:23:09.990 --> 00:23:12.210
But we need a social movement

00:23:12.210 --> 00:23:16.768
somehow tantamount to
the right wing's attack

00:23:16.768 --> 00:23:18.090
on public schools to fight back

00:23:18.090 --> 00:23:20.610
so that we can begin to
have a debate about this,

00:23:20.610 --> 00:23:23.343
not just on Ron DeSantis's terms.

00:23:24.390 --> 00:23:26.873
And I hope that's the last
time I used his name here.

00:23:29.614 --> 00:23:31.290
- All right, well, thanks
for those initial snapshots.

00:23:31.290 --> 00:23:33.750
I think they helped give
us a different, right,

00:23:33.750 --> 00:23:36.420
putting together like a
different pieces of the puzzle

00:23:36.420 --> 00:23:37.670
that people had accessed.

00:23:39.019 --> 00:23:41.040
I actually want to pick
up then on something,

00:23:41.040 --> 00:23:42.840
Professor Blight that you just said.

00:23:43.918 --> 00:23:46.396
I think one of the
complexities of this decision

00:23:46.396 --> 00:23:48.600
to offer what Professor Ferguson described

00:23:48.600 --> 00:23:52.380
as a certain articulation
and version of the course

00:23:52.380 --> 00:23:57.030
is that it allows the governor
and the Board of Education

00:23:57.030 --> 00:24:01.140
to state that they are not
actually explicitly opposed

00:24:01.140 --> 00:24:03.960
to teaching Black history
in Florida classrooms,

00:24:03.960 --> 00:24:06.060
but that they have an obligation to ensure

00:24:07.093 --> 00:24:10.050
that the content is, in their
words, educationally valuable.

00:24:10.050 --> 00:24:11.596
So they're actually,

00:24:11.596 --> 00:24:15.114
they have a kind of
disavowal at stake, right?

00:24:15.114 --> 00:24:17.955
And it's also the case

00:24:17.955 --> 00:24:20.094
that there have been
some prominent scholars

00:24:20.094 --> 00:24:20.927
through different avenues
that have signed off

00:24:20.927 --> 00:24:22.983
on this version of the course,

00:24:24.976 --> 00:24:27.180
arguing that's either an
incremental opportunity

00:24:27.180 --> 00:24:30.390
to introduce important
material to students

00:24:30.390 --> 00:24:33.150
or that the content that was left out

00:24:33.150 --> 00:24:34.833
was secondary to the curriculum.

00:24:35.726 --> 00:24:38.134
And this gets to something,
David, that you said that,

00:24:38.134 --> 00:24:40.443
that it was theory as opposed to history.

00:24:41.693 --> 00:24:43.890
And so it's a kind of
non-ideological decision,

00:24:43.890 --> 00:24:47.253
colorblind decision to privilege
some content over others.

00:24:48.780 --> 00:24:51.068
So for the next round,

00:24:51.068 --> 00:24:52.560
I wanted to think about
how you kind of unpack

00:24:52.560 --> 00:24:56.040
and make sense of these
comments, these disavowals.

00:24:56.040 --> 00:24:58.353
This has nothing to do with X.

00:25:00.000 --> 00:25:02.280
What do they bring to mind for you?

00:25:02.280 --> 00:25:05.250
What are the underlying
links between the scholars,

00:25:05.250 --> 00:25:07.680
and I think this is really
important for us to think about,

00:25:07.680 --> 00:25:11.790
the scholars named and
the sets of politics

00:25:11.790 --> 00:25:15.420
and analytics and prisms that
were at stake in their work.

00:25:15.420 --> 00:25:19.350
Because the official defense
is we had room for 40,

00:25:19.350 --> 00:25:21.720
we had, you know, there were 60 there.

00:25:21.720 --> 00:25:23.970
We just, this is a product of compromise.

00:25:23.970 --> 00:25:26.430
It has no politics attached to it.

00:25:26.430 --> 00:25:29.070
So I actually wanna just bring this back.

00:25:29.070 --> 00:25:31.627
Let's start with you, David, a little bit

00:25:31.627 --> 00:25:34.083
to think about the histories of disavowal.

00:25:35.220 --> 00:25:38.106
What's familiar perhaps about this,

00:25:38.106 --> 00:25:40.200
this sense that it has nothing
to do with the content,

00:25:40.200 --> 00:25:41.940
with the political commitments

00:25:41.940 --> 00:25:44.583
or with race and producing
racial knowledge itself?

00:25:47.760 --> 00:25:49.310
- I have to go first this time?

00:25:50.445 --> 00:25:51.293
It's more fun to go last,

00:25:52.130 --> 00:25:55.265
but every time you hear somebody say

00:25:55.265 --> 00:25:57.563
this is not about politics,
it's about politics.

00:25:59.091 --> 00:26:00.687
I mean, it is.

00:26:00.687 --> 00:26:02.940
In fact, that's what
this is ultimately about.

00:26:02.940 --> 00:26:05.520
It is ultimately about the elimination

00:26:05.520 --> 00:26:07.690
of Roderick and Kim's work

00:26:08.709 --> 00:26:11.610
and that and Ta-Nehisi's
essay on reparations

00:26:11.610 --> 00:26:15.841
and the unit on Black Lives Matter

00:26:15.841 --> 00:26:19.653
and the other six or seven
items that were removed.

00:26:20.987 --> 00:26:23.461
But this is a political discussion.

00:26:23.461 --> 00:26:24.461
This is about power.

00:26:25.544 --> 00:26:27.090
This is about who gets
to control education.

00:26:27.090 --> 00:26:29.689
And it's way beyond education.

00:26:29.689 --> 00:26:31.929
It's about who gets to
control the next election,

00:26:31.929 --> 00:26:33.579
who gets to control legislatures.

00:26:35.192 --> 00:26:39.003
So anytime they're, and the
idea that the College Board,

00:26:39.861 --> 00:26:40.694
come on folks, and I actually,

00:26:40.694 --> 00:26:42.840
there's a lot of good
people who work there.

00:26:42.840 --> 00:26:47.010
They, I mean, you may not like
these AP tests and courses

00:26:47.010 --> 00:26:49.737
and God help you, but, you know,

00:26:51.076 --> 00:26:52.414
they work hard on these things.

00:26:52.414 --> 00:26:54.897
It's also a billion dollar operation.

00:26:55.836 --> 00:26:57.636
Let's remember that for a nonprofit.

00:27:01.618 --> 00:27:05.552
But they are making political decisions.

00:27:05.552 --> 00:27:06.752
It's really that simple.

00:27:07.713 --> 00:27:11.710
Now this business of
what's a secondary source

00:27:11.710 --> 00:27:15.063
and a primary source here
is a false distinction.

00:27:16.717 --> 00:27:18.900
I mean, my goodness, your work is now,

00:27:18.900 --> 00:27:20.190
it's a primary source.

00:27:20.190 --> 00:27:21.033
Good lord.

00:27:22.193 --> 00:27:24.720
I mean in a real sense it's become,

00:27:24.720 --> 00:27:28.710
there are ways in which
what's written about something

00:27:28.710 --> 00:27:31.740
in a time, in a moment becomes over time

00:27:31.740 --> 00:27:34.050
its own kind of primary source.

00:27:34.050 --> 00:27:36.639
I mean, when Dubois wrote the last chapter

00:27:36.639 --> 00:27:38.400
of "Black Reconstruction,
the Propaganda of History,"

00:27:38.400 --> 00:27:41.340
that was the last chapter
of black reconstruction.

00:27:41.340 --> 00:27:44.610
That is now very much a primary source.

00:27:44.610 --> 00:27:46.050
And I've been teaching it all my life.

00:27:46.050 --> 00:27:46.883
Okay.

00:27:48.180 --> 00:27:49.358
Does that mean I have two minutes?

00:27:49.358 --> 00:27:50.191
Oh, cool.

00:27:51.460 --> 00:27:55.500
But so that's just a false distinction,

00:27:55.500 --> 00:27:57.120
and they made that up

00:27:57.120 --> 00:27:59.070
in the absence of something else to say.

00:27:59.070 --> 00:28:01.518
We just cut out secondary sources.

00:28:01.518 --> 00:28:02.693
No, they didn't.

00:28:02.693 --> 00:28:05.950
There are secondary sources
earlier in the course as well.

00:28:05.950 --> 00:28:06.990
And by the way, there's a lot that's good

00:28:06.990 --> 00:28:10.320
in those first three
quarters of this course.

00:28:10.320 --> 00:28:13.350
They spend the first
five weeks of this course

00:28:13.350 --> 00:28:15.930
on African civilizations,
African societies,

00:28:15.930 --> 00:28:18.450
the origins of the African slave trade,

00:28:18.450 --> 00:28:20.580
the beginnings of an African diaspora

00:28:20.580 --> 00:28:22.650
and all of its enormous complexity.

00:28:22.650 --> 00:28:25.860
How many American kids
know anything about that?

00:28:25.860 --> 00:28:29.618
It's fantastic, and they
get five weeks on it.

00:28:29.618 --> 00:28:30.614
That's good.

00:28:30.614 --> 00:28:31.862
Celebrate that.

00:28:31.862 --> 00:28:34.950
But they made choices to
take out what's contemporary

00:28:34.950 --> 00:28:39.003
because clearly the they,

00:28:39.892 --> 00:28:41.520
whoever was making all these
choices in the College Board

00:28:41.520 --> 00:28:43.863
saw this as where the
controversy would be.

00:28:45.552 --> 00:28:47.141
And I don't know exactly when.

00:28:47.141 --> 00:28:47.974
Somebody will ferret this out.

00:28:47.974 --> 00:28:50.940
There's a journalist out who's
gonna figure all this out.

00:28:50.940 --> 00:28:54.537
Maybe even a historian
about who did this and when,

00:28:54.537 --> 00:28:59.140
you know, in 2022, and
when did they really decide

00:28:59.140 --> 00:29:00.660
to take critical race theory out.

00:29:00.660 --> 00:29:01.717
I don't know.

00:29:01.717 --> 00:29:04.290
It was in originally and then it's not.

00:29:04.290 --> 00:29:07.833
So also last point, last point.

00:29:10.620 --> 00:29:15.060
Look, what I wanna know is
how many teachers were asked,

00:29:15.060 --> 00:29:18.140
and there were teachers involved
in this process, I'm told.

00:29:18.140 --> 00:29:20.130
But how many, I don't know.

00:29:20.130 --> 00:29:21.450
Maybe Mira, you know,

00:29:21.450 --> 00:29:24.210
how many actual high school teachers

00:29:24.210 --> 00:29:27.630
are involved in the
creation of these courses?

00:29:27.630 --> 00:29:30.990
How many teachers weighed
in on what they teach

00:29:30.990 --> 00:29:34.473
or what they want to teach or
what they know how to teach?

00:29:35.322 --> 00:29:36.990
That would be very valuable to know.

00:29:36.990 --> 00:29:39.450
Cause they're the people
who have to do it,

00:29:39.450 --> 00:29:42.525
and they do have
discretion in how to teach.

00:29:42.525 --> 00:29:45.210
They don't all teach exactly
the same thing on the same day.

00:29:45.210 --> 00:29:47.793
Teachers have discretion
in these programs.

00:29:48.685 --> 00:29:49.864
And we shouldn't talk about

00:29:49.864 --> 00:29:52.042
this AP African-American Studies course

00:29:52.042 --> 00:29:54.030
as if it's a curriculum from on high

00:29:54.030 --> 00:29:55.893
that everybody must do the same.

00:29:57.300 --> 00:29:59.550
But it is true, they made a choice

00:29:59.550 --> 00:30:03.840
to make about eight or nine
of these units at the end

00:30:03.840 --> 00:30:06.543
optional for research papers.

00:30:08.220 --> 00:30:10.118
Now it could just happen

00:30:10.118 --> 00:30:11.490
that young people are so
interested in this stuff,

00:30:11.490 --> 00:30:13.380
they're all gonna write
their papers on it.

00:30:13.380 --> 00:30:14.213
Who knows.

00:30:16.920 --> 00:30:17.753
- Thanks, David.

00:30:18.628 --> 00:30:20.282
I mean that, and that
does raise questions then

00:30:20.282 --> 00:30:22.380
about teachers that would choose
to foreground those issues

00:30:22.380 --> 00:30:23.583
more intentionally.

00:30:24.581 --> 00:30:25.699
- They might, many might.

00:30:25.699 --> 00:30:27.109
- The vulnerability, right?

00:30:27.109 --> 00:30:28.868
There's a kind of like,
you've been warned.

00:30:28.868 --> 00:30:30.860
- Some states especially, yes.

00:30:30.860 --> 00:30:32.370
- And particularly for teachers
of color and Black teachers

00:30:32.370 --> 00:30:36.330
who are much more likely to
be viewed with suspicion.

00:30:36.330 --> 00:30:39.630
You know, don't you know we
laid out the directions here,

00:30:39.630 --> 00:30:42.660
and the possibility that
one may have discretion

00:30:42.660 --> 00:30:44.160
over some parts, right?

00:30:44.160 --> 00:30:47.740
What text to use in introducing, right,

00:30:47.740 --> 00:30:49.297
kind of early abolitionist thought.

00:30:49.297 --> 00:30:50.965
You know, there, but here.

00:30:50.965 --> 00:30:52.140
- Especially in the two dozen states

00:30:52.140 --> 00:30:55.164
that have divisive concept laws,

00:30:55.164 --> 00:30:57.014
- Right, working with it there, yeah.

00:30:58.403 --> 00:30:59.236
- Teaching under a divisive concept law.

00:31:00.395 --> 00:31:02.351
- Right, yeah, and how that changes

00:31:02.351 --> 00:31:03.732
the working conditions of teachers.

00:31:03.732 --> 00:31:04.911
- Yeah, right.

00:31:04.911 --> 00:31:06.630
- At a moment of profound
need in public education.

00:31:06.630 --> 00:31:08.556
Jason, let's turn to you.

00:31:08.556 --> 00:31:09.690
I mean, part of this is also, you know,

00:31:09.690 --> 00:31:11.580
history is about kind of facts.

00:31:11.580 --> 00:31:13.353
Yeah, it's just events.

00:31:14.410 --> 00:31:17.014
And what we don't wanna do
is impose something, right?

00:31:17.014 --> 00:31:19.764
There is a kind of theory-less
history, so, well, yeah,

00:31:20.731 --> 00:31:22.535
what's your kind of take on that

00:31:22.535 --> 00:31:25.050
and how that allows us to understand

00:31:25.050 --> 00:31:27.603
or not organize our sense of the past?

00:31:28.851 --> 00:31:31.050
- Yeah, so no philosopher of history

00:31:31.050 --> 00:31:34.590
would ever say history is
just a series of facts.

00:31:34.590 --> 00:31:37.350
History is a narrative
structured by a narrative.

00:31:37.350 --> 00:31:40.200
That's basic to the philosophy of history.

00:31:40.200 --> 00:31:45.200
So the quest is, so really it's
all philosophy when you get.

00:31:47.218 --> 00:31:50.850
(cross talking and chuckling)

00:31:50.850 --> 00:31:53.760
So you have to ask what narrative

00:31:53.760 --> 00:31:55.710
are they trying to eliminate?

00:31:55.710 --> 00:31:58.500
What narrative are they targeting?

00:31:58.500 --> 00:32:00.870
And the narrative they're targeting

00:32:00.870 --> 00:32:04.200
is the narrative that
drew me as a philosopher

00:32:04.200 --> 00:32:07.770
into the study of recent US history.

00:32:07.770 --> 00:32:10.410
For me as a philosopher,

00:32:10.410 --> 00:32:14.193
and this is the main topic of my 2015 book

00:32:14.193 --> 00:32:15.026
"How Propaganda Works,"

00:32:15.026 --> 00:32:18.300
I wanted to know how after
the civil rights movement,

00:32:18.300 --> 00:32:21.150
after this movement for liberation,

00:32:21.150 --> 00:32:24.180
racial injustice completely persisted.

00:32:24.180 --> 00:32:28.532
Mass incarceration,
what we now incarcerate,

00:32:28.532 --> 00:32:31.171
200,000 people are serving life sentences

00:32:31.171 --> 00:32:33.120
that's basically roughly
the full prison system

00:32:33.120 --> 00:32:34.740
when I was born.

00:32:34.740 --> 00:32:39.540
So how after the civil rights movement

00:32:39.540 --> 00:32:42.300
in a country that lauds
the civil rights movement,

00:32:42.300 --> 00:32:45.180
do we end up with resegregation?

00:32:45.180 --> 00:32:48.270
In 2014, 2015, last
time I spun the numbers,

00:32:48.270 --> 00:32:51.330
almost 10% of the
world's prison population

00:32:51.330 --> 00:32:53.670
came from the formerly enslaved population

00:32:53.670 --> 00:32:54.840
of the United States.

00:32:54.840 --> 00:32:57.660
So the theorists they're targeting

00:32:57.660 --> 00:33:00.607
are the ones that theorize about that.

00:33:00.607 --> 00:33:04.440
The theorists they're targeting
are the ones that say, wait,

00:33:04.440 --> 00:33:08.534
the civil rights movement
didn't solve all the problems.

00:33:08.534 --> 00:33:11.250
And so that's what they're trying to do.

00:33:11.250 --> 00:33:13.800
They're trying to say, we
solved racial injustice.

00:33:13.800 --> 00:33:16.980
Martin Luther King, that's
why we've got a holiday.

00:33:16.980 --> 00:33:20.690
And that's very clearly
what they're trying to do.

00:33:20.690 --> 00:33:23.730
They're trying to eliminate any studies

00:33:23.730 --> 00:33:27.123
of the practices and
structures of the institutions,

00:33:27.990 --> 00:33:31.653
of the police, of the
criminal legal system,

00:33:32.520 --> 00:33:35.310
of the school system that results,

00:33:35.310 --> 00:33:40.310
of banking that keeps
the racial wealth gap

00:33:40.920 --> 00:33:44.580
basically what it was before
the Civil rights movement.

00:33:44.580 --> 00:33:47.596
Yale's, my colleague Jennifer Richardson,

00:33:47.596 --> 00:33:49.436
from whose work I've learned so much,

00:33:49.436 --> 00:33:52.655
studies the myth of the
myth of racial progress,

00:33:52.655 --> 00:33:55.560
the myth of the narrative
of racial progress.

00:33:55.560 --> 00:33:58.440
White Americans think that Black Americans

00:33:58.440 --> 00:34:03.150
have 90% of the wealth of white Americans

00:34:03.150 --> 00:34:05.400
when they have 10% of the wealth.

00:34:05.400 --> 00:34:08.375
So what they're trying to do

00:34:08.375 --> 00:34:10.560
is they're trying to make inexplicable

00:34:10.560 --> 00:34:13.140
Black movements for social justice.

00:34:13.140 --> 00:34:15.780
They're trying to make it so
that when you see a protest,

00:34:15.780 --> 00:34:17.790
you're like what are
they complaining about?

00:34:17.790 --> 00:34:20.490
We have Martin Luther King Day.

00:34:20.490 --> 00:34:25.490
So it's very clear to me
what they're trying to do.

00:34:25.552 --> 00:34:27.270
They're trying to remove all structural,

00:34:27.270 --> 00:34:32.270
any kind of study of structures
and practices that persist

00:34:32.760 --> 00:34:34.533
and make us think

00:34:34.533 --> 00:34:37.260
that because we have Martin
Luther King Day, we did it.

00:34:37.260 --> 00:34:39.420
So, and finally, I wanna note,

00:34:39.420 --> 00:34:44.215
I wanna end on this question
of no political content,

00:34:44.215 --> 00:34:45.928
which as David said, you know,

00:34:45.928 --> 00:34:47.040
the most political content of all

00:34:47.040 --> 00:34:49.770
is saying that you have
no political content.

00:34:49.770 --> 00:34:53.288
So as you all know from your
arguments in your dorms,

00:34:53.288 --> 00:34:56.940
but suppose that somebody said,

00:34:56.940 --> 00:34:59.389
we're going to make illegal

00:34:59.389 --> 00:35:00.983
the concept of sexual harassment.

00:35:02.547 --> 00:35:06.733
Suppose someone said, and suppose
like 18 states passed laws

00:35:06.733 --> 00:35:10.994
making it illegal to teach the
concept of sexual harassment.

00:35:10.994 --> 00:35:12.180
You couldn't mention it,
you couldn't talk about it.

00:35:12.180 --> 00:35:16.860
Nobody would be fooled about
what's going on, right?

00:35:16.860 --> 00:35:21.000
So why has there not been an outcry

00:35:21.000 --> 00:35:23.193
about the concept of intersectionality?

00:35:24.120 --> 00:35:26.460
Sexual harassment affects black women,

00:35:26.460 --> 00:35:27.960
white women, all women.

00:35:27.960 --> 00:35:32.013
Intersectionality is only
about a subset of women.

00:35:32.940 --> 00:35:36.750
Is it because it's about that subset

00:35:36.750 --> 00:35:39.663
that there there isn't the
outrage that there should be?

00:35:40.866 --> 00:35:41.985
- Great, thanks Jason.

00:35:41.985 --> 00:35:43.223
And I think there's, you know,
just a couple things there.

00:35:44.192 --> 00:35:45.731
One is saying there's a clear relationship

00:35:45.731 --> 00:35:47.370
between the content of a history course

00:35:47.370 --> 00:35:51.495
and how you understand what's
natural or not in the world.

00:35:51.495 --> 00:35:54.780
It orients your expectations,
your desires, your hopes.

00:35:54.780 --> 00:35:57.510
It very much allows you to
discern or not discern things.

00:35:57.510 --> 00:35:59.160
That's partly what's at stake.

00:35:59.160 --> 00:36:03.120
And the taking away of a
particular kind of framework

00:36:03.120 --> 00:36:07.260
actually denies, right, a
whole subset of students,

00:36:07.260 --> 00:36:10.806
you know, Baldwin in his, you
know, Talk to Teachers Essay,

00:36:10.806 --> 00:36:12.091
says this.

00:36:12.091 --> 00:36:13.490
Students see everything.

00:36:13.490 --> 00:36:14.323
They see the inequalities.

00:36:14.323 --> 00:36:15.690
They see the humiliation.

00:36:15.690 --> 00:36:17.520
They, you know, they
see the possibilities.

00:36:17.520 --> 00:36:21.000
They come to classroom
asking what's happening here?

00:36:21.000 --> 00:36:24.253
And the notion that a
teacher would be told,

00:36:24.253 --> 00:36:26.645
we can't talk about that.

00:36:26.645 --> 00:36:30.660
Or what you're seeing is
just as your other example,

00:36:30.660 --> 00:36:32.374
it's just random.

00:36:32.374 --> 00:36:34.410
If you see someone you know, humiliated,

00:36:34.410 --> 00:36:36.090
it's likely their fault.

00:36:36.090 --> 00:36:39.820
Yeah, so you're, you
know, and that produces

00:36:40.729 --> 00:36:42.550
a certain kind of understanding,

00:36:42.550 --> 00:36:44.431
and that's not power neutral in that way.

00:36:44.431 --> 00:36:45.480
So that's really helpful.

00:36:45.480 --> 00:36:47.163
Rod, let's turn to you now.

00:36:48.060 --> 00:36:50.580
I'm also very much
thinking about your work

00:36:50.580 --> 00:36:53.250
on student movements
and reminding us about

00:36:53.250 --> 00:36:56.650
the capacious imagination
they had for these classes

00:36:57.655 --> 00:36:59.588
and how you might put that into dialogue

00:36:59.588 --> 00:37:00.473
with what we're seeing now.

00:37:02.229 --> 00:37:03.062
- Yeah, I mean, you know,

00:37:04.007 --> 00:37:09.007
if we think about the
student protest movements,

00:37:09.230 --> 00:37:11.580
but also just the general
social protest movements

00:37:11.580 --> 00:37:16.580
at the end of the 60s,
the beginning of the 70s,

00:37:16.834 --> 00:37:18.693
you know, what we saw actually,

00:37:19.627 --> 00:37:22.535
and contrary to the popular narrative

00:37:22.535 --> 00:37:25.420
is that the Black Panthers
were in conversation

00:37:26.349 --> 00:37:27.743
with the gay liberation folks.

00:37:28.589 --> 00:37:30.930
Marcia B. Johnson, Sylvia Rivera,

00:37:30.930 --> 00:37:34.030
the members of Street Transvestite
Action Revolutionaries

00:37:35.370 --> 00:37:39.540
in conversation with Huey P. Newton.

00:37:39.540 --> 00:37:42.469
Newton writing the communique

00:37:42.469 --> 00:37:44.457
about women's and gay liberation, right.

00:37:44.457 --> 00:37:48.431
The didactics house in Philadelphia,

00:37:48.431 --> 00:37:51.480
learning from and producing
their own kind of socialist,

00:37:51.480 --> 00:37:54.630
lesbian, anti-racist, feminism,

00:37:54.630 --> 00:37:56.310
the Combahee River Collective.

00:37:56.310 --> 00:37:59.730
So all of these groups were in
conversation with each other,

00:37:59.730 --> 00:38:01.230
being inspired by each other,

00:38:01.230 --> 00:38:03.210
trying to learn from one another

00:38:03.210 --> 00:38:08.210
and also then producing a kind
of cartography of struggle.

00:38:08.370 --> 00:38:13.370
Right, and here we are now with an edict

00:38:14.490 --> 00:38:16.983
to abolish those cartographies, right?

00:38:18.710 --> 00:38:20.683
This is what that's about, right?

00:38:20.683 --> 00:38:23.743
It's the reason that
we're targeted, you know,

00:38:23.743 --> 00:38:27.324
that our colleagues,
Angela Davis, you know,

00:38:27.324 --> 00:38:29.835
Robin, Kelly targeted.

00:38:29.835 --> 00:38:30.988
It's because, you know,

00:38:30.988 --> 00:38:33.030
people have been working
to produce cartographies

00:38:33.030 --> 00:38:34.330
so that we can make links.

00:38:35.283 --> 00:38:36.938
That's what intersectionality is.

00:38:36.938 --> 00:38:40.770
You know, how do I think about the links

00:38:40.770 --> 00:38:43.380
between modes of difference

00:38:43.380 --> 00:38:47.520
and histories that are
supposed to be disconnected

00:38:47.520 --> 00:38:48.573
from one another?

00:38:50.161 --> 00:38:51.783
That's the threat, right?

00:38:52.785 --> 00:38:53.618
That's the word.

00:38:54.544 --> 00:38:57.150
That people are actually
producing frameworks

00:38:57.150 --> 00:39:01.050
by which they can see the
links between things, right?

00:39:01.050 --> 00:39:06.050
That they're not discreet
entities, that there are overlaps.

00:39:06.540 --> 00:39:08.103
That there are affinities.

00:39:09.060 --> 00:39:13.320
And if we do that, then there
might be the possibility

00:39:13.320 --> 00:39:17.940
that there could be strange
affinities, unlikely alliances,

00:39:17.940 --> 00:39:20.283
you know, between those groups.

00:39:21.345 --> 00:39:22.487
You know?

00:39:22.487 --> 00:39:25.090
That's what strikes terror

00:39:26.100 --> 00:39:29.673
in the hearts of these folks, okay.

00:39:32.061 --> 00:39:33.570
- Yeah, I just, Rod, that's
just so important and moving

00:39:33.570 --> 00:39:36.462
because I think it also pushes us past,

00:39:36.462 --> 00:39:38.435
so we can say there's an edict,

00:39:38.435 --> 00:39:39.720
I think is a helpful term here, right?

00:39:39.720 --> 00:39:41.373
Because their disavowing edict,

00:39:42.519 --> 00:39:45.761
but the elimination of a queer
presence in the curriculum,

00:39:45.761 --> 00:39:48.420
a feminist intersectional,
one could read as well,

00:39:48.420 --> 00:39:51.123
that's a kind of, it's simply
bias, and they're just,

00:39:52.123 --> 00:39:53.279
they need to be more open to it.

00:39:53.279 --> 00:39:54.390
But you're saying it's not just that.

00:39:54.390 --> 00:39:55.980
It's not just, you know,

00:39:55.980 --> 00:39:59.490
because one could even imagine
incorporation of a presence

00:39:59.490 --> 00:40:01.742
that's not doing that work there.

00:40:01.742 --> 00:40:02.790
So there's something much more at stake

00:40:02.790 --> 00:40:07.199
in the kind of purging of
those bodies of knowledge.

00:40:07.199 --> 00:40:09.921
It never happened, and it's not
something you have available

00:40:09.921 --> 00:40:10.754
to think with.

00:40:10.754 --> 00:40:12.330
Okay, Kim, let's turn to you now

00:40:12.330 --> 00:40:14.700
to think also about this
question of history.

00:40:14.700 --> 00:40:17.850
Actually, I just wanna
intersperse a comment from Daisha.

00:40:17.850 --> 00:40:20.888
So Daisha Brabham, who was
supposed to be on the panel,

00:40:20.888 --> 00:40:22.602
is a Windsor high school teacher.

00:40:22.602 --> 00:40:24.900
She teaches a Black and
latino studies course

00:40:24.900 --> 00:40:26.670
in Connecticut.

00:40:26.670 --> 00:40:28.500
And she wrote in preparation

00:40:28.500 --> 00:40:31.027
after she realized she
wasn't able to come.

00:40:31.027 --> 00:40:33.667
"One immediate effect in the
classroom of these policies

00:40:33.667 --> 00:40:36.370
"is to further distort students' reality

00:40:37.280 --> 00:40:38.360
"of the past and present.

00:40:38.360 --> 00:40:40.421
"One of the rationales put
forward by the College Board

00:40:40.421 --> 00:40:42.758
"is that by taking away the
more theoretical aspects

00:40:42.758 --> 00:40:44.099
"of the curriculum.

00:40:44.099 --> 00:40:46.260
"But that same rationale
is not being applied

00:40:46.260 --> 00:40:48.667
"to the ideas of James Madison
and Alexander Hamilton,

00:40:48.667 --> 00:40:50.827
"who are also producing
theories on government.

00:40:50.827 --> 00:40:53.527
"The product of this is to imply further

00:40:53.527 --> 00:40:55.777
"that Black people are not contributors

00:40:55.777 --> 00:40:57.903
"or capable of theory or thought.

00:40:58.782 --> 00:41:02.062
"One of my students recently wrote a paper

00:41:02.062 --> 00:41:03.847
"comparing Crenshaw's
ideas on intersectionality

00:41:03.847 --> 00:41:06.217
"to another 20th century thinker.

00:41:06.217 --> 00:41:07.807
"These exercises are essential

00:41:07.807 --> 00:41:11.118
"if you're going to teach
about James Madison's ideas

00:41:11.118 --> 00:41:12.367
"on democracy, then it's equally essential

00:41:12.367 --> 00:41:15.777
"to consider Angela Davis's
ideas on abolition democracy."

00:41:18.085 --> 00:41:21.210
So I, you know, and you
talked about this today,

00:41:21.210 --> 00:41:23.598
in your, about public health,

00:41:23.598 --> 00:41:28.440
about the kind of re-energizing of ideas

00:41:28.440 --> 00:41:31.290
about racial superiority and inferiority

00:41:31.290 --> 00:41:34.246
that come about in these
disavowed settings.

00:41:34.246 --> 00:41:36.387
But let's turn to you
now to kind of help us

00:41:36.387 --> 00:41:37.720
think about these histories.

00:41:37.720 --> 00:41:39.930
- Yeah, well, and let me add,

00:41:39.930 --> 00:41:43.860
the assumption is also
that students can't learn

00:41:43.860 --> 00:41:45.030
about democracy.

00:41:45.030 --> 00:41:48.060
They can't learn about the government.

00:41:48.060 --> 00:41:51.090
They can't learn about the
state from the experiences

00:41:51.090 --> 00:41:52.050
that prompted them

00:41:52.050 --> 00:41:54.423
to want to have this
course in the first place.

00:41:55.381 --> 00:41:58.920
So some of the irony, I guess
we might call it in the story,

00:41:58.920 --> 00:42:01.200
is that the very conditions of possibility

00:42:01.200 --> 00:42:06.200
that opened up this course,
the aftermath of George Floyd

00:42:06.990 --> 00:42:08.950
and Breonna Taylor,

00:42:08.950 --> 00:42:13.500
the contemporary dimensions
of the histories,

00:42:13.500 --> 00:42:15.990
that's precisely the information

00:42:15.990 --> 00:42:17.910
that's precisely the thinking

00:42:17.910 --> 00:42:20.897
that has now been rendered optional.

00:42:20.897 --> 00:42:23.460
And I just wanna say, as far
as teachers are concerned,

00:42:23.460 --> 00:42:26.730
what this also does is to tell teachers

00:42:26.730 --> 00:42:30.240
you can teach this at your own peril.

00:42:30.240 --> 00:42:33.480
It deprives them of the ability to say,

00:42:33.480 --> 00:42:36.240
no, this is part of the AP curriculum.

00:42:36.240 --> 00:42:38.280
So it's effectively saying to teachers

00:42:38.280 --> 00:42:40.560
who have now been subjected to

00:42:40.560 --> 00:42:44.040
in many of these states
that have memory laws,

00:42:44.040 --> 00:42:48.660
surveillance in the classroom, fines,

00:42:48.660 --> 00:42:52.470
the possibility of being
sued, losing their jobs,

00:42:52.470 --> 00:42:54.660
and then beyond that losing their license.

00:42:54.660 --> 00:42:57.990
It's not just losing their
jobs but losing their license.

00:42:57.990 --> 00:43:01.620
So what this has done is deprive them

00:43:01.620 --> 00:43:04.830
of some institutional capacity to say,

00:43:04.830 --> 00:43:06.870
what I am doing is authorized.

00:43:06.870 --> 00:43:08.820
What I am doing is legitimate.

00:43:08.820 --> 00:43:12.230
What I am doing is important
to the educational project.

00:43:12.230 --> 00:43:15.480
So I wanna say that
part of the conversation

00:43:15.480 --> 00:43:17.880
about when did it happen,

00:43:17.880 --> 00:43:20.700
whether the teachers were consulted,

00:43:20.700 --> 00:43:24.300
there is an interesting problem

00:43:24.300 --> 00:43:25.950
in whether they've been consulted

00:43:25.950 --> 00:43:29.490
because right now the course
has not been fully taught,

00:43:29.490 --> 00:43:31.320
the information that has to,

00:43:31.320 --> 00:43:33.099
they're halfway through.

00:43:33.099 --> 00:43:35.100
So what are you gonna
consult the teachers about,

00:43:35.100 --> 00:43:36.660
about the stuff that's taken out

00:43:36.660 --> 00:43:39.780
because they haven't even had
an opportunity to teach it.

00:43:39.780 --> 00:43:42.360
In law that's called, you know,

00:43:42.360 --> 00:43:45.210
sort of contradictory evidence, right?

00:43:45.210 --> 00:43:50.190
But what I wanna suggest
is even our focus on

00:43:50.190 --> 00:43:52.650
did they do it a couple of weeks ago

00:43:52.650 --> 00:43:54.450
or have they done it for a whole year

00:43:54.450 --> 00:43:56.280
is a problematic framework

00:43:56.280 --> 00:43:59.790
cause it makes it seem as
though the damage that's done

00:43:59.790 --> 00:44:03.210
is only damage if someone
intended it to be done.

00:44:03.210 --> 00:44:06.031
It's only damage if it's intended to be

00:44:06.031 --> 00:44:08.880
in response to DeSantis's
claims last week.

00:44:08.880 --> 00:44:10.800
But in critical race theory,

00:44:10.800 --> 00:44:13.500
one of the things we do is
suggest that damage is done,

00:44:13.500 --> 00:44:15.480
whether it's intended or not.

00:44:15.480 --> 00:44:19.350
Damage is done whether it
happened last week or last year.

00:44:19.350 --> 00:44:21.090
Damage is done, right?

00:44:21.090 --> 00:44:23.910
Because effectively what this has created

00:44:23.910 --> 00:44:26.310
is a course that basically
gives the students

00:44:26.310 --> 00:44:28.260
letters from ancient history,

00:44:28.260 --> 00:44:30.330
but doesn't give them the
grammar to put it together

00:44:30.330 --> 00:44:32.370
into meaning in the here and now.

00:44:32.370 --> 00:44:34.620
So what does it mean to say, yeah,

00:44:34.620 --> 00:44:36.210
we had a lot of history in it,

00:44:36.210 --> 00:44:38.250
but we decided not to say anything

00:44:38.250 --> 00:44:40.920
or not to make the
movement for Black Lives

00:44:40.920 --> 00:44:45.000
a legitimate topic that
is required in the course.

00:44:45.000 --> 00:44:46.440
What does it mean to say,

00:44:46.440 --> 00:44:50.555
okay, we might have talked a
little bit about World War II

00:44:50.555 --> 00:44:53.340
and maybe we talked about
the Federal Housing Act

00:44:53.340 --> 00:44:58.140
depriving Black GIS of
the same kind of access

00:44:58.140 --> 00:45:00.870
to housing in suburbs.

00:45:00.870 --> 00:45:04.320
Maybe it meant that they
didn't get the same opportunity

00:45:04.320 --> 00:45:06.091
as white people did.

00:45:06.091 --> 00:45:08.160
But what we're not talking
about is that deprivation

00:45:08.160 --> 00:45:11.280
of that opportunity in the 40s, in the 50s

00:45:11.280 --> 00:45:15.510
has now ballooned into
billions of dollars of wealth

00:45:15.510 --> 00:45:19.230
that white GIS were able to
get and pass on to their kids,

00:45:19.230 --> 00:45:23.580
that white GIS were able to
pass on that Black GIS weren't.

00:45:23.580 --> 00:45:24.960
Our students in this course

00:45:24.960 --> 00:45:27.390
won't be able to really talk about that

00:45:27.390 --> 00:45:30.720
because that has to do with
contemporary inequalities.

00:45:30.720 --> 00:45:33.810
And the whole point of
this attack is to say,

00:45:33.810 --> 00:45:36.750
we can talk about history
as much as we want,

00:45:36.750 --> 00:45:38.640
but where we're drawing the line

00:45:38.640 --> 00:45:41.320
is in saying that that has anything to do

00:45:41.320 --> 00:45:42.535
with the here and now.

00:45:42.535 --> 00:45:44.160
Because if it has something
to do with the here and now,

00:45:44.160 --> 00:45:47.310
it implicates what people need to be doing

00:45:47.310 --> 00:45:50.137
in the here and now to really make good

00:45:50.137 --> 00:45:51.300
on the promises of equity and inclusion.

00:45:51.300 --> 00:45:54.180
And that's precisely
what divisive concepts

00:45:54.180 --> 00:45:56.070
are basically against.

00:45:56.070 --> 00:46:00.113
So I just wanna say
that part of this debate

00:46:01.260 --> 00:46:06.260
that they're having is kind
of affirming the very ideas

00:46:06.810 --> 00:46:09.780
that they don't want in it
in the first place, right?

00:46:09.780 --> 00:46:12.660
They're affirming the
idea that it only counts

00:46:12.660 --> 00:46:15.630
if we did it because DeSantis told us to.

00:46:15.630 --> 00:46:19.650
Not it counts because we are
depriving a new generation

00:46:19.650 --> 00:46:20.910
of the education they need

00:46:20.910 --> 00:46:23.393
to understand the lives
that they've been born into.

00:46:24.636 --> 00:46:27.803
(audience applauding)

00:46:33.660 --> 00:46:34.493
- I think,

00:46:35.678 --> 00:46:38.334
and I just want to bring
in Daisha one more time,

00:46:38.334 --> 00:46:40.014
and then we'll go to the third part

00:46:40.014 --> 00:46:42.098
because it speaks, you
know, directly to that,

00:46:42.098 --> 00:46:44.298
the question of what historical knowledge

00:46:44.298 --> 00:46:48.036
can be mobilized to do
outside of just events.

00:46:48.036 --> 00:46:49.539
And so Daisha comments,

00:46:49.539 --> 00:46:52.417
"Carol Anderson, a really
important historian's book

00:46:52.417 --> 00:46:54.760
"'White Rage' is essential for us to see

00:46:55.699 --> 00:46:57.367
"that these attacks are not
new, but should be expected.

00:46:57.367 --> 00:47:00.513
"Behind every civil rights
gain lies an attack.

00:47:00.513 --> 00:47:02.377
"The fact that many are
unaware of the struggle

00:47:02.377 --> 00:47:04.837
"behind the aftermath of Brown V Board

00:47:04.837 --> 00:47:06.920
"or the removal of troops

00:47:06.920 --> 00:47:08.818
"after the reconstruction amendments

00:47:08.818 --> 00:47:11.523
"demonstrate yet another
indictment on the education system

00:47:11.523 --> 00:47:13.417
"that we are consistently
not telling the whole story.

00:47:13.417 --> 00:47:15.999
"Treating these wins as the products

00:47:15.999 --> 00:47:19.360
"of individual characters
or inevitable success

00:47:19.360 --> 00:47:20.527
"when the truth is that it was the product

00:47:20.527 --> 00:47:23.007
"of decades worth of
organizing in communities."

00:47:23.859 --> 00:47:25.978
So I think it's a just
another astute way of saying

00:47:25.978 --> 00:47:27.660
you can actually take the
same kind of like line

00:47:27.660 --> 00:47:29.550
on the timeline event,

00:47:29.550 --> 00:47:31.680
and the framework with
which it's discussed

00:47:31.680 --> 00:47:35.295
and engaged with students makes
all the difference, right?

00:47:35.295 --> 00:47:36.128
All the difference.

00:47:37.775 --> 00:47:39.097
We're not gonna go to our last round,

00:47:39.097 --> 00:47:41.130
and we'll continue to invite
you to put your questions there

00:47:41.130 --> 00:47:43.170
as we put those together.

00:47:43.170 --> 00:47:44.433
Yeah, we have those here,

00:47:46.057 --> 00:47:48.333
and here we're trying
to really think about

00:47:48.333 --> 00:47:49.223
where do we go from here.

00:47:50.672 --> 00:47:53.842
So I'd like each of you
to think about your sense

00:47:53.842 --> 00:47:56.433
of two roads that might
lead from this moment.

00:47:57.917 --> 00:48:00.960
The first in which this
decision is simply endorsed,

00:48:00.960 --> 00:48:03.720
accepted, maybe some hand wringing,

00:48:03.720 --> 00:48:05.733
but otherwise with little objection,

00:48:07.209 --> 00:48:09.107
either because it's pragmatic to do.

00:48:09.107 --> 00:48:11.388
Simply we have to accept the conditions

00:48:11.388 --> 00:48:13.066
or it's just not worth the fight.

00:48:13.066 --> 00:48:15.816
It's actually not, it's not
relevant to other concerns.

00:48:16.760 --> 00:48:19.530
What happens, and what
danger does it promise

00:48:19.530 --> 00:48:22.203
beyond Florida high schools?

00:48:24.148 --> 00:48:26.550
The second in which we
see this as a moment

00:48:26.550 --> 00:48:29.163
that requires concerted
and collective action.

00:48:30.086 --> 00:48:31.080
What might that action look like?

00:48:31.080 --> 00:48:33.730
And what kinds of futures
could we potentially build?

00:48:34.650 --> 00:48:37.724
And for this, Rod, we're
gonna turn to you first,

00:48:37.724 --> 00:48:39.474
if that's all right.

00:48:41.162 --> 00:48:45.386
- One object to it, right?

00:48:45.386 --> 00:48:46.920
Simply object to it.

00:48:46.920 --> 00:48:48.030
As you're objecting to it,

00:48:48.030 --> 00:48:51.866
go back to Martin Luther King's letter

00:48:51.866 --> 00:48:53.580
from the Birmingham Jail, okay?

00:48:53.580 --> 00:48:58.580
In that letter, he defines,
theorizes direct action.

00:49:01.524 --> 00:49:04.230
All right, and says why
direct action is needed.

00:49:04.230 --> 00:49:09.230
When a society refuses to come to grips

00:49:09.300 --> 00:49:11.850
with its historical moment,

00:49:11.850 --> 00:49:14.703
a creative tension has to be produced.

00:49:15.630 --> 00:49:17.670
The creative tension can be produced

00:49:17.670 --> 00:49:20.640
through collective mobilization, right?

00:49:20.640 --> 00:49:24.510
The use of the body, the
use of culture forms,

00:49:24.510 --> 00:49:25.773
culture practices.

00:49:26.918 --> 00:49:30.153
Read that and then do
something with it, okay?

00:49:31.050 --> 00:49:34.859
Ironically, the other thing that we can do

00:49:34.859 --> 00:49:39.859
that the DeSantis folks say they want,

00:49:41.190 --> 00:49:42.840
that will actually go a long way,

00:49:44.669 --> 00:49:47.403
read and write, okay?

00:49:48.281 --> 00:49:50.273
So the stuff that they don't
want you to read, read it.

00:49:51.588 --> 00:49:56.588
Okay, the things that they
are saying should not be read.

00:49:56.910 --> 00:50:01.803
Imagine that as a list of
recommendations, you know?

00:50:03.047 --> 00:50:04.597
As a syllabus in and of itself.

00:50:05.526 --> 00:50:08.670
You know, pass those onto
other people, all right?

00:50:08.670 --> 00:50:13.410
Produce the classroom outside
of the classroom, right?

00:50:13.410 --> 00:50:18.410
In your dorms, in your
clubs, with your families.

00:50:19.446 --> 00:50:24.446
All right, let's take back that
moment when it was illegal,

00:50:26.006 --> 00:50:28.270
for instance, for black people,

00:50:28.270 --> 00:50:31.080
black enslaved people
to read and to write.

00:50:31.080 --> 00:50:32.070
So what did they do?

00:50:32.070 --> 00:50:36.060
They had things like
midnight schools, all right,

00:50:36.060 --> 00:50:39.822
where they produced critical
education, you know,

00:50:39.822 --> 00:50:44.822
away from, you know, the
master's purview, right?

00:50:45.900 --> 00:50:49.320
Produce those, multiply them.

00:50:49.320 --> 00:50:53.370
And also demand that that curriculum

00:50:53.370 --> 00:50:58.370
belongs also in public education,
you know, in K through 12.

00:50:59.010 --> 00:51:03.605
And that it also, it belongs
in university settings

00:51:03.605 --> 00:51:05.940
because that's what's coming next.

00:51:05.940 --> 00:51:08.850
You know, don't be lulled into thinking

00:51:08.850 --> 00:51:12.303
that this will just stay
with K through 12 teachers.

00:51:13.364 --> 00:51:18.040
Okay, you know, the other
target will be higher education.

00:51:19.023 --> 00:51:22.042
We already see that with DeSantis saying

00:51:22.042 --> 00:51:23.400
that he is going to go after

00:51:23.400 --> 00:51:28.280
diversity, equity, and
inclusion units, you know,

00:51:28.280 --> 00:51:30.184
within colleges and
universities, all right?

00:51:30.184 --> 00:51:31.556
So that's what's at stake.

00:51:31.556 --> 00:51:34.110
Multiply the instances and moments

00:51:34.110 --> 00:51:36.453
and context for critical education.

00:51:37.448 --> 00:51:40.248
Get involved, produce your
own direct action, all right?

00:51:42.307 --> 00:51:44.003
It only takes a handful
of folks, you know.

00:51:45.825 --> 00:51:46.973
What did Audre Lorde say?

00:51:47.840 --> 00:51:51.123
Find your work and do it, period.

00:51:52.382 --> 00:51:55.549
(audience applauding)

00:51:59.850 --> 00:52:01.045
- Thanks, Rod.

00:52:01.045 --> 00:52:02.340
Before we go to Jason, I
just wanna underscore right,

00:52:02.340 --> 00:52:06.690
of course the notion that there
might be some robust version

00:52:06.690 --> 00:52:09.720
of African American studies,
ethnic studies at Yale

00:52:09.720 --> 00:52:13.800
at another place, even as
the rest of the, you know,

00:52:13.800 --> 00:52:15.600
that is no ethnic studies,
that is no, right?

00:52:15.600 --> 00:52:17.520
It's completely antithetical.

00:52:17.520 --> 00:52:22.106
And even for in higher ed, you know,

00:52:22.106 --> 00:52:25.170
graduate students pursuing
teaching and research jobs,

00:52:25.170 --> 00:52:27.450
you know, teachers wanting
to go into the field,

00:52:27.450 --> 00:52:29.525
all of this will affect you.

00:52:29.525 --> 00:52:30.900
People who are working
on abolition, right?

00:52:30.900 --> 00:52:33.960
All of these topics have
now been subject to censure.

00:52:33.960 --> 00:52:36.360
I never thought in 15 years

00:52:36.360 --> 00:52:39.030
I would work with a graduate
student in these fields

00:52:39.030 --> 00:52:40.705
and advise them

00:52:40.705 --> 00:52:42.150
before they give their
ethnic studies job talk,

00:52:42.150 --> 00:52:45.300
they might have to tone down the politics

00:52:45.300 --> 00:52:46.917
and the transformation.

00:52:46.917 --> 00:52:49.560
The whole purpose was
that this was the field

00:52:49.560 --> 00:52:50.910
that these things could be,

00:52:51.763 --> 00:52:54.027
where the knowledge
could be produced, right?

00:52:54.027 --> 00:52:55.560
So the fact that they're now saying

00:52:55.560 --> 00:52:57.750
that the discipline is coming here.

00:52:57.750 --> 00:52:59.133
No one is exempt from that.

00:53:00.060 --> 00:53:03.101
Jason, let's turn to you now
in that sense, two roads,

00:53:03.101 --> 00:53:06.150
what happens if we don't act

00:53:06.150 --> 00:53:08.150
and what are the possibilities if we do?

00:53:09.363 --> 00:53:11.628
- Yeah, I hate to be Debbie Downer,

00:53:11.628 --> 00:53:14.430
but that's kind of my work.

00:53:14.430 --> 00:53:18.000
What happens if we don't take

00:53:18.000 --> 00:53:20.523
the kind of route Rod suggested?

00:53:21.969 --> 00:53:23.768
I think, I think Danny,

00:53:23.768 --> 00:53:24.990
you phrased it a little
too optimistically.

00:53:24.990 --> 00:53:27.660
Like you were like, oh, well, you know,

00:53:27.660 --> 00:53:31.200
if it's just ethnic
studies being free at Yale,

00:53:31.200 --> 00:53:34.650
but it's not free elsewhere
in public universities,

00:53:34.650 --> 00:53:37.740
then you know, it still
will affect us indirectly.

00:53:37.740 --> 00:53:40.533
No, it'll come for Yale too.

00:53:41.890 --> 00:53:44.790
It'll, like that's one thing
if you look internationally,

00:53:44.790 --> 00:53:46.440
you might think, oh, they'll just stop

00:53:46.440 --> 00:53:47.760
at the public universities.

00:53:47.760 --> 00:53:50.865
And it's already well beyond K through 12.

00:53:50.865 --> 00:53:54.300
Texas Lieutenant Governor
said he's gonna fire

00:53:54.300 --> 00:53:56.940
all the University of
Texas at Austin faculty

00:53:56.940 --> 00:53:59.931
who teach critical race theory, who teach,

00:53:59.931 --> 00:54:02.370
I taught, Tim Snyder and I
taught Angela Davis on Tuesday

00:54:02.370 --> 00:54:03.870
in our class.

00:54:03.870 --> 00:54:06.063
So that would be illegal.

00:54:06.063 --> 00:54:09.254
We'd be fire-able for the
University of Texas and Austin,

00:54:09.254 --> 00:54:12.000
which is supposed to
be a great university.

00:54:12.000 --> 00:54:14.160
So it's already coming for public schools,

00:54:14.160 --> 00:54:17.590
public universities, and we
can, and you might think, okay,

00:54:17.590 --> 00:54:19.530
but we're at private
universities, we're safe.

00:54:19.530 --> 00:54:23.370
Na-uh, Orbán, Viktor
Orbán already figured out

00:54:23.370 --> 00:54:25.260
how to deal with that problem.

00:54:25.260 --> 00:54:30.260
He got Central European University
using various legalities

00:54:31.080 --> 00:54:34.628
to close up shop, the best
university in Hungary,

00:54:34.628 --> 00:54:35.461
a private university.

00:54:35.461 --> 00:54:40.113
He got them to close up
shop and move to Vienna.

00:54:42.030 --> 00:54:45.131
So, and they're imitating dissent.

00:54:45.131 --> 00:54:46.274
They're dissenters.

00:54:46.274 --> 00:54:49.020
They're imitating Orbán
because Orbán was the speaker

00:54:49.020 --> 00:54:51.873
at the keynote at the National
Conservatives Conference.

00:54:52.770 --> 00:54:55.320
And he was very skilled
at taking over the courts

00:54:55.320 --> 00:54:58.620
and targeting private concerns.

00:54:58.620 --> 00:55:02.250
He forced the whole free press
to sell itself to his friends

00:55:02.250 --> 00:55:06.150
by suddenly everybody
faced enormous tax bills.

00:55:06.150 --> 00:55:09.090
And if you listen to JD Vance,

00:55:09.090 --> 00:55:12.277
whose talk at the National
Conservatives Conference was

00:55:12.277 --> 00:55:17.277
"Our Enemy is the Professors,"
I believe it was called.

00:55:18.708 --> 00:55:22.532
The idea was that they'll get,

00:55:22.532 --> 00:55:24.609
they're theorizing very explicitly

00:55:24.609 --> 00:55:26.370
how to go after the private universities.

00:55:26.370 --> 00:55:28.350
They'll go after our endowments.

00:55:28.350 --> 00:55:31.675
They'll go after, well,
I'm in favor of that, but.

00:55:31.675 --> 00:55:33.213
(audience laughing)

00:55:33.213 --> 00:55:38.213
They'll go after, yeah, they'll
go after federal funding.

00:55:39.690 --> 00:55:40.740
They'll go after,

00:55:40.740 --> 00:55:44.220
there's a number of ways
they can play with tax codes.

00:55:44.220 --> 00:55:47.670
There's a number of ways
they they can imitate

00:55:47.670 --> 00:55:50.040
what's happened in Eastern Europe here

00:55:50.040 --> 00:55:51.030
with privates as well.

00:55:51.030 --> 00:55:53.670
So it's gonna, they're
gonna come after everybody.

00:55:53.670 --> 00:55:56.970
And this is a corporation too.

00:55:56.970 --> 00:55:59.220
And so, just like the College Board,

00:55:59.220 --> 00:56:04.220
they care about profits here
too, so don't be fooled.

00:56:05.992 --> 00:56:08.142
So the goal is, as
Professor Crenshaw said,

00:56:09.111 --> 00:56:10.980
that you look at present
events and be baffled.

00:56:10.980 --> 00:56:14.490
So that's their end goal,
and what they're doing,

00:56:14.490 --> 00:56:17.580
if you look at what we let
happen in the United States,

00:56:17.580 --> 00:56:20.460
there's a book called
"Laboratories of Autocracy"

00:56:20.460 --> 00:56:24.360
about states like
Wisconsin, North Carolina,

00:56:24.360 --> 00:56:26.100
where basically the
United States was like,

00:56:26.100 --> 00:56:29.567
okay, let's have a little
Hungary inside the United States

00:56:29.567 --> 00:56:31.590
where the state, if the
democratic governor wins,

00:56:31.590 --> 00:56:32.670
the state legislature,

00:56:32.670 --> 00:56:34.890
gerrymandered in state legislature

00:56:34.890 --> 00:56:37.500
will vote to strip the
governor of his powers.

00:56:37.500 --> 00:56:40.452
Every time they have a
democratic governor in Wisconsin

00:56:40.452 --> 00:56:41.460
and North Carolina, that's what they do.

00:56:41.460 --> 00:56:42.870
And at first everyone was outraged,

00:56:42.870 --> 00:56:45.514
and everyone's like, okay,
that's just Wisconsin

00:56:45.514 --> 00:56:46.768
and North Carolina.

00:56:46.768 --> 00:56:50.553
We can't let this happen
because once you let it happen,

00:56:50.553 --> 00:56:52.950
you know, everyone's like,
well, it's just like that state.

00:56:52.950 --> 00:56:54.210
It's just another state.

00:56:54.210 --> 00:56:57.480
So we've done it already
with state houses.

00:56:57.480 --> 00:56:59.010
We've done it with gerrymandering.

00:56:59.010 --> 00:57:01.203
We've done it with our congressional,

00:57:02.151 --> 00:57:03.090
our congress people.

00:57:03.090 --> 00:57:05.043
North Carolina gerrymandered maps.

00:57:05.931 --> 00:57:07.353
They're trying to, you know,

00:57:07.353 --> 00:57:10.770
50/50 vote Democrat and
Republican, but you know, you know,

00:57:10.770 --> 00:57:14.640
75% of their representative
Congress people are Republicans.

00:57:14.640 --> 00:57:16.310
So we can't let it happen with education.

00:57:16.310 --> 00:57:21.023
- Great, thank you, Jason.
(audience applauding)

00:57:22.289 --> 00:57:23.796
Again, remind you to look at the,

00:57:23.796 --> 00:57:26.620
to message text your question in

00:57:26.620 --> 00:57:28.837
so that we have about
15 minutes remaining.

00:57:28.837 --> 00:57:32.070
So before we get to David
and Kim to close us out,

00:57:32.070 --> 00:57:35.093
just gonna bring Daisha in one
more time on this last point.

00:57:35.093 --> 00:57:38.017
Who says, "Although the
recent attacks of CRT

00:57:38.017 --> 00:57:41.295
"are making mainstream,
there are more silent attacks

00:57:41.295 --> 00:57:43.477
"happening every day in the
underfunding of schools,

00:57:43.477 --> 00:57:46.653
"the removal of elective
courses for students

00:57:46.653 --> 00:57:47.797
"that often expose them to classes

00:57:47.797 --> 00:57:50.197
"such as AP African-American
history being removed

00:57:50.197 --> 00:57:53.055
"because of a lack of staff, et cetera.

00:57:53.055 --> 00:57:55.076
"The attack on education is far reaching,

00:57:55.076 --> 00:57:57.578
"and we cannot lose in this debate."

00:57:57.578 --> 00:57:59.278
And then "We must now remain vigilant

00:57:59.278 --> 00:58:01.837
"and use the community
framework to work together,

00:58:01.837 --> 00:58:04.567
"collective action,
community activist projects

00:58:04.567 --> 00:58:08.393
"to address the educational
malpractice we see in Florida

00:58:08.393 --> 00:58:09.226
"and across the country.

00:58:09.226 --> 00:58:11.196
"In the words of James Brown,

00:58:11.196 --> 00:58:14.516
"we need to get up, get
into it, and get involved.

00:58:14.516 --> 00:58:16.897
"Parents, students,
teachers, administrators

00:58:16.897 --> 00:58:19.267
"and families in the community together

00:58:19.267 --> 00:58:22.720
"to mediate and strategize the
short and long-term effects

00:58:23.557 --> 00:58:25.309
"of this attack.

00:58:25.309 --> 00:58:27.180
"We need an all hands on deck strategy."

00:58:27.180 --> 00:58:28.140
And I just wanna say,

00:58:28.140 --> 00:58:29.820
it's again to the point of like teachers

00:58:29.820 --> 00:58:32.454
who are experiencing this most acutely

00:58:32.454 --> 00:58:35.020
and profoundly understand
the landscape and see it

00:58:36.210 --> 00:58:38.640
as a matter of academic freedom for sure,

00:58:38.640 --> 00:58:41.400
but also deeply tied to
many other transformations

00:58:41.400 --> 00:58:43.710
that they and their students experience.

00:58:43.710 --> 00:58:46.680
David, we're gonna turn to
you and then finish with Kim.

00:58:46.680 --> 00:58:47.943
Two roads, which way?

00:58:49.389 --> 00:58:51.083
- Well, your first road is not an option.

00:58:52.249 --> 00:58:53.890
I think we're all saying that.

00:58:53.890 --> 00:58:54.723
And Daisha, if you're watching,

00:58:54.723 --> 00:58:55.860
thanks for setting me up

00:58:55.860 --> 00:58:57.860
cause I wanna talk about public schools.

00:58:59.400 --> 00:59:02.610
A lot of us met Daisha last
fall at our conference here

00:59:02.610 --> 00:59:04.834
that the Gilder Lehrman Center did

00:59:04.834 --> 00:59:06.993
about teaching slavery and race.

00:59:08.352 --> 00:59:11.403
And we celebrated
teachers to say the least.

00:59:12.300 --> 00:59:14.150
I'll come back to the public schools.

00:59:16.380 --> 00:59:19.950
You know, and I wanna endorse,

00:59:19.950 --> 00:59:22.156
well, I wanna endorse
the David Pepper book

00:59:22.156 --> 00:59:24.292
that you mentioned about
state legislatures.

00:59:24.292 --> 00:59:26.876
That's an extremely
important book to read.

00:59:26.876 --> 00:59:27.874
If you wanna understand

00:59:27.874 --> 00:59:29.160
what's really happened
to American politics.

00:59:29.160 --> 00:59:31.530
The action is in the states, folks.

00:59:31.530 --> 00:59:33.180
It's not as sexy.

00:59:33.180 --> 00:59:36.003
Run for state legislature,
isn't that exciting?

00:59:37.740 --> 00:59:40.503
You want an interview
with a state legislature?

00:59:41.520 --> 00:59:42.423
No, you don't.

00:59:43.304 --> 00:59:44.483
You want an interview with an iBank?

00:59:45.510 --> 00:59:48.853
Sorry. (audience laughing)

00:59:48.853 --> 00:59:51.270
Anyway, but we need you
in the state legislature

00:59:51.270 --> 00:59:54.027
and all right, all right, enough of that.

00:59:54.027 --> 00:59:55.403
But that book by David
Pepper is brilliant.

00:59:56.649 --> 00:59:59.520
First of all, I went back,
I'll do this real quickly.

00:59:59.520 --> 01:00:01.020
One thing we need to know here

01:00:02.148 --> 01:00:05.643
is that African American
studies is a deep old tradition.

01:00:07.412 --> 01:00:11.960
It's 55 to 60 years old as
a curriculum phenomenon.

01:00:13.140 --> 01:00:16.032
This is a little bit memory
lane, and it makes me old,

01:00:16.032 --> 01:00:21.032
which I am, but I first taught
an AFAM intro course in 1988

01:00:23.880 --> 01:00:25.980
at Harvard, where I spent two years.

01:00:25.980 --> 01:00:28.023
We were just inventing a new version.

01:00:28.890 --> 01:00:32.010
And that summer of 1988,

01:00:32.010 --> 01:00:35.130
I was privileged to be part of an NEH

01:00:35.130 --> 01:00:38.040
like three week workshop

01:00:38.040 --> 01:00:40.560
on how to create introductory courses

01:00:40.560 --> 01:00:42.090
in African-American studies.

01:00:42.090 --> 01:00:45.600
It was, and I just dug out a
whole bunch of my old files.

01:00:45.600 --> 01:00:46.473
It was fun.

01:00:48.029 --> 01:00:49.929
We had experts, most of them now dead.

01:00:51.170 --> 01:00:53.787
We had James Gibbs, the anthropologist.

01:00:53.787 --> 01:00:56.520
We had Al Raboteau, the great
historian of Black religion.

01:00:56.520 --> 01:00:58.320
We had all kinds of people come.

01:00:58.320 --> 01:01:00.450
We had one gentleman, Julius Wilson, come

01:01:00.450 --> 01:01:04.350
and spend a whole day talking
about urban sociology,

01:01:04.350 --> 01:01:06.220
on and, we had Hazel Carby come

01:01:07.070 --> 01:01:08.490
and talk about literature and women.

01:01:08.490 --> 01:01:10.233
It was phenomenal.

01:01:12.033 --> 01:01:15.330
And what we learned is
this whole field began

01:01:15.330 --> 01:01:16.752
really in anthropology.

01:01:16.752 --> 01:01:18.480
It began in the great
debates over Herskovitz,

01:01:18.480 --> 01:01:20.806
and E. Franklin Frazier.

01:01:20.806 --> 01:01:24.496
And what's African about
African-American culture

01:01:24.496 --> 01:01:26.347
which is a never ending debate.

01:01:26.347 --> 01:01:27.900
It's fascinating stuff.

01:01:27.900 --> 01:01:31.110
We ended with a whole long
unit on Black literature,

01:01:31.110 --> 01:01:33.510
and in the middle, an incredible unit.

01:01:33.510 --> 01:01:36.210
Unit, we spent days on this,

01:01:36.210 --> 01:01:38.283
on the historiography of slavery,

01:01:39.607 --> 01:01:43.409
which circa late 80s was still
booming in a certain way.

01:01:43.409 --> 01:01:44.242
It's still booming.

01:01:45.354 --> 01:01:46.560
And we created courses.

01:01:46.560 --> 01:01:49.293
You'd be amazed at the
readings on these courses.

01:01:50.610 --> 01:01:52.653
The tradition is so deep,

01:01:54.108 --> 01:01:55.290
and sometimes you just
need to tell people that.

01:01:55.290 --> 01:01:56.710
This isn't brand new

01:01:57.573 --> 01:02:00.312
just because the AP is
creating a course on it

01:02:00.312 --> 01:02:01.352
for god's sake.

01:02:01.352 --> 01:02:02.723
And then I moved to Amherst College

01:02:02.723 --> 01:02:04.475
where we had Black Studies 11,

01:02:04.475 --> 01:02:05.880
which was our Introduction
to Black Studies.

01:02:05.880 --> 01:02:07.023
We team taught it.

01:02:08.186 --> 01:02:10.170
It was one of the most fun experiences

01:02:10.170 --> 01:02:13.320
I've ever had teaching with
like three colleagues at once.

01:02:13.320 --> 01:02:15.150
We had to have a historian, a philosopher,

01:02:15.150 --> 01:02:18.960
and a literary critic
all weighing in on this.

01:02:18.960 --> 01:02:20.943
And my goodness, it was rich.

01:02:21.792 --> 01:02:24.060
Sometimes we'd have students
in the class who, you know,

01:02:24.060 --> 01:02:26.273
they'd have to read this and read that,

01:02:26.273 --> 01:02:28.171
and it was intensive reading.

01:02:28.171 --> 01:02:30.244
And they'd say, can we just talk about

01:02:30.244 --> 01:02:31.343
what it's like to be Black at Amherst?

01:02:33.071 --> 01:02:35.193
No, we'd say, do the reading.

01:02:36.300 --> 01:02:38.820
We can talk about that later if you want.

01:02:38.820 --> 01:02:41.670
But you know, you all know that situation.

01:02:41.670 --> 01:02:44.013
Here's the syllabus, but here's my life.

01:02:45.410 --> 01:02:46.770
And we found that we had to put

01:02:46.770 --> 01:02:48.593
a little more time in on that sometimes.

01:02:50.100 --> 01:02:53.835
Anyway, I'll spare you any
more of this memory lane.

01:02:53.835 --> 01:02:55.890
But this is a deep old tradition,

01:02:55.890 --> 01:02:59.103
and it's gone through all kinds
of permutations and changes.

01:03:00.630 --> 01:03:01.888
Last point.

01:03:01.888 --> 01:03:03.603
Good, I got a minute.

01:03:04.530 --> 01:03:06.000
Roderick is absolutely right.

01:03:06.000 --> 01:03:08.433
Organize politically.

01:03:10.115 --> 01:03:12.750
This is a political question of power.

01:03:12.750 --> 01:03:15.360
We need a social movement
in the United States,

01:03:15.360 --> 01:03:18.660
tantamount to Black Lives
Matter and even bigger.

01:03:18.660 --> 01:03:22.500
It needs Soros level of funding and more

01:03:22.500 --> 01:03:27.500
to save, preserve, enhance
the public schools.

01:03:27.540 --> 01:03:30.090
85% of Americans are
educated in a public school.

01:03:30.090 --> 01:03:31.800
I went to nothing but public schools.

01:03:31.800 --> 01:03:33.900
I taught seven years in
a public high school.

01:03:33.900 --> 01:03:36.690
I have no education
without public schools.

01:03:36.690 --> 01:03:38.700
The public school is the
most democratic thing

01:03:38.700 --> 01:03:41.743
the United States ever invented.

01:03:41.743 --> 01:03:43.162
(audience applauding)

01:03:43.162 --> 01:03:48.077
And there is a multi-billion
dollar crusade to destroy them.

01:03:49.741 --> 01:03:52.562
(someone quietly talking)

01:03:52.562 --> 01:03:53.562
- Yeah, I mean look.

01:03:54.505 --> 01:03:56.672
- [Woman] Say that louder!

01:03:57.610 --> 01:03:59.050
- By enslaved black people,

01:03:59.050 --> 01:04:01.653
that's where the public
education system comes from.

01:04:03.423 --> 01:04:04.770
- Well, it does in great parts.

01:04:04.770 --> 01:04:06.109
They were.

01:04:06.109 --> 01:04:07.625
- Just go ahead and read.

01:04:07.625 --> 01:04:09.708
- Yeah, yeah, yeah, no,
it does, it does, it does.

01:04:09.708 --> 01:04:11.782
Although they were trying
to create these things

01:04:11.782 --> 01:04:12.615
in Massachusetts earlier too,

01:04:12.615 --> 01:04:13.448
and they did.

01:04:13.448 --> 01:04:16.830
One state mandated public
schools before the Civil War,

01:04:16.830 --> 01:04:17.673
so you're right.

01:04:18.949 --> 01:04:21.784
But hey, you want a new social movement?

01:04:21.784 --> 01:04:25.383
Save the public schools and go teach.

01:04:26.728 --> 01:04:29.895
(audience applauding)

01:04:36.109 --> 01:04:40.192
- All right, Kim, we're
gonna get some, you know,

01:04:41.444 --> 01:04:43.867
questions here, but yeah,
we're at this moment.

01:04:43.867 --> 01:04:46.848
We've heard what's at stake,

01:04:46.848 --> 01:04:48.150
and we've heard generally
and broadly, you know,

01:04:48.150 --> 01:04:49.988
where we need to go.

01:04:49.988 --> 01:04:51.420
But I'm hoping you can also help us

01:04:51.420 --> 01:04:53.489
kind of deepen that a little bit

01:04:53.489 --> 01:04:57.210
and talk about both and certainly
related to the APF work,

01:04:57.210 --> 01:05:00.093
what are the campaigns,
what are the possibilities,

01:05:01.840 --> 01:05:03.042
the alliances that help to build that?

01:05:03.042 --> 01:05:06.300
- Yeah, so I wanna start
where David left off

01:05:06.300 --> 01:05:09.004
and raise a question.

01:05:09.004 --> 01:05:12.243
This campaign that we've been part,

01:05:13.102 --> 01:05:16.590
that we've been witnessing
over the last couple of years,

01:05:16.590 --> 01:05:21.590
it hasn't been mysterious
about what its objectives were.

01:05:22.817 --> 01:05:24.090
They've said straight up,

01:05:24.090 --> 01:05:29.090
they're hoping to sow
dissatisfaction with public education

01:05:29.246 --> 01:05:30.570
in order to dismantle it.

01:05:30.570 --> 01:05:33.780
And they're starting with
the frameworks that they know

01:05:33.780 --> 01:05:36.302
people are most ambivalent about

01:05:36.302 --> 01:05:39.390
and the people that people feel
less concerned with, right?

01:05:39.390 --> 01:05:43.350
So, where has everybody
been for the last two years

01:05:43.350 --> 01:05:45.930
with this divisive concepts idea?

01:05:45.930 --> 01:05:48.420
Where has everybody been with the attack

01:05:48.420 --> 01:05:50.400
on critical race theory?

01:05:50.400 --> 01:05:53.760
Where has everybody been in this notion

01:05:53.760 --> 01:05:58.556
that education is meant
to make people feel good?

01:05:58.556 --> 01:05:59.910
This is like MAGA education.

01:05:59.910 --> 01:06:03.000
To make America great
again, you've gotta believe

01:06:03.000 --> 01:06:05.490
that it used to be great for everybody.

01:06:05.490 --> 01:06:07.320
And the only way you can sustain that

01:06:07.320 --> 01:06:10.260
is to erase the parts of the
history that were not great.

01:06:10.260 --> 01:06:13.980
Where has everybody been
while this is going on?

01:06:13.980 --> 01:06:15.820
It's not about what happened last week.

01:06:15.820 --> 01:06:18.540
It is about what has been generated

01:06:18.540 --> 01:06:21.660
for the last two years at minimum.

01:06:21.660 --> 01:06:25.260
So what it makes me question

01:06:25.260 --> 01:06:28.740
is whether we are moving
to another moment,

01:06:28.740 --> 01:06:31.830
which I call race McCarthyism,

01:06:31.830 --> 01:06:35.820
where there's a set of ideas
that people say are toxic,

01:06:35.820 --> 01:06:39.510
radioactive, anyone that has
anything to do with them,

01:06:39.510 --> 01:06:42.540
anyone who cites anyone that
has anything to do with them,

01:06:42.540 --> 01:06:47.540
anyone who reads those ideas,
they are dangerous people.

01:06:47.640 --> 01:06:52.640
And the rest of us will sometimes
just disavow those ideas

01:06:55.200 --> 01:06:56.703
and those people.

01:06:56.703 --> 01:06:58.500
So think about what the
conversation has been

01:06:58.500 --> 01:07:00.343
for the last two years.

01:07:00.343 --> 01:07:02.430
When it's come up, is critical
race theory being taught?

01:07:02.430 --> 01:07:04.530
Most people are like, oh, no, no, no.

01:07:04.530 --> 01:07:06.600
We're not teaching critical race theory.

01:07:06.600 --> 01:07:09.570
Now you actually look at
what they're actually saying

01:07:09.570 --> 01:07:10.650
and doing.

01:07:10.650 --> 01:07:13.479
They're, exactly.

01:07:13.479 --> 01:07:17.550
It's a matter of what it's called, right?

01:07:17.550 --> 01:07:19.262
But here's the thing.

01:07:19.262 --> 01:07:21.930
You cannot outrun what it is

01:07:21.930 --> 01:07:24.030
because what they're after is not the name

01:07:24.030 --> 01:07:25.740
but the set of ideas.

01:07:25.740 --> 01:07:27.840
So if you're teaching those ideas,

01:07:27.840 --> 01:07:30.600
if you're talking about structural racism,

01:07:30.600 --> 01:07:32.904
you're not gonna be saved by saying,

01:07:32.904 --> 01:07:34.230
but it's not critical race theory.

01:07:34.230 --> 01:07:39.230
You're saying that current
inequality is in part the product

01:07:39.780 --> 01:07:44.670
of the past that is neutrally
institutionalized everywhere.

01:07:44.670 --> 01:07:47.155
You're not gonna be saved by it.

01:07:47.155 --> 01:07:48.270
Once they get done with people

01:07:48.270 --> 01:07:50.550
who are clearly critical race theorists,

01:07:50.550 --> 01:07:53.880
our history tells us they
come after the next group.

01:07:53.880 --> 01:07:56.910
All history tells us they
come after the next group,

01:07:56.910 --> 01:07:59.310
American history, European history,

01:07:59.310 --> 01:08:01.770
you can broaden it to
other histories as well.

01:08:01.770 --> 01:08:03.600
So we know this,

01:08:03.600 --> 01:08:06.570
but we're not acting
as though we know this.

01:08:06.570 --> 01:08:08.639
And I wanna ask the question why.

01:08:08.639 --> 01:08:13.260
Is it because it's about
difficult conversations

01:08:13.260 --> 01:08:15.540
that everybody has a hard time with?

01:08:15.540 --> 01:08:18.030
So there's a little bit
of, yeah, you know what?

01:08:18.030 --> 01:08:20.340
That kind of race stuff
can get kind of hard.

01:08:20.340 --> 01:08:22.560
Maybe we shouldn't be talking about it.

01:08:22.560 --> 01:08:25.050
Maybe there really is a there there

01:08:25.050 --> 01:08:27.419
that I don't have to worry about

01:08:27.419 --> 01:08:28.320
because it's just about that.

01:08:28.320 --> 01:08:31.110
Whatever people thought
up until last week,

01:08:31.110 --> 01:08:33.480
they should not be thinking it anymore.

01:08:33.480 --> 01:08:36.030
Because it's abundantly
clear that this isn't about

01:08:36.030 --> 01:08:38.490
whether you call it critical
race theory, intersectionality.

01:08:38.490 --> 01:08:42.390
It's about education that
allows people to understand

01:08:42.390 --> 01:08:43.980
the historical groundwork

01:08:43.980 --> 01:08:46.290
upon which this contemporary society

01:08:46.290 --> 01:08:49.260
and all of its inequalities are based.

01:08:49.260 --> 01:08:52.980
And if we cannot name that,
if we cannot talk about it,

01:08:52.980 --> 01:08:55.560
if we cannot frame it,

01:08:55.560 --> 01:08:58.470
whether it's through theory or narrative,

01:08:58.470 --> 01:09:00.030
we are unable to change it.

01:09:00.030 --> 01:09:02.970
And that is exactly the point.

01:09:02.970 --> 01:09:05.139
So now that we know that,

01:09:05.139 --> 01:09:08.483
now that it's clear what
they're going after,

01:09:08.483 --> 01:09:09.780
and let's be clear, DeSantis also said

01:09:09.780 --> 01:09:12.480
he's going after diversity,
equity, and inclusion

01:09:12.480 --> 01:09:13.773
on campuses.

01:09:14.642 --> 01:09:15.840
He's going after courses.

01:09:15.840 --> 01:09:20.700
He's going after professors
who he doesn't want to testify,

01:09:20.700 --> 01:09:24.630
use their knowledge to
testify in law cases

01:09:24.630 --> 01:09:26.190
around civil rights.

01:09:26.190 --> 01:09:28.740
So this is a total takeover

01:09:28.740 --> 01:09:31.620
of the knowledge producing industry.

01:09:31.620 --> 01:09:35.104
So what are we going
to do about it, right?

01:09:35.104 --> 01:09:36.990
Now I will say that one of the things

01:09:36.990 --> 01:09:39.300
that has been surprising to me

01:09:39.300 --> 01:09:44.300
is how little colleges,
presidents, administrators,

01:09:44.700 --> 01:09:47.880
have thought that they needed
to say anything about this.

01:09:47.880 --> 01:09:49.440
Like, where are the presidents?

01:09:49.440 --> 01:09:53.580
Where are the people to say
it's time to do something

01:09:53.580 --> 01:09:55.800
and defend our institution

01:09:55.800 --> 01:09:58.413
and the project that we have been about.

01:09:59.583 --> 01:10:01.530
Now, what I know, what
I've heard, what I've seen

01:10:01.530 --> 01:10:04.680
is that there's a
reluctance to get involved,

01:10:04.680 --> 01:10:07.770
a reluctance to be seen as political,

01:10:07.770 --> 01:10:10.380
but it's kind of like, you know,

01:10:10.380 --> 01:10:13.500
when someone's coming
to burn down your house,

01:10:13.500 --> 01:10:15.540
for you to say I'm not
gonna throw some water on it

01:10:15.540 --> 01:10:19.080
because that would be
political, that's just suicide.

01:10:19.080 --> 01:10:20.640
When people are coming after

01:10:20.640 --> 01:10:23.550
the very mission of your institution,

01:10:23.550 --> 01:10:26.760
for you not to defend it
is to basically give them

01:10:26.760 --> 01:10:28.170
the keys to the house.

01:10:28.170 --> 01:10:30.873
And that's effectively
what I think is happening.

01:10:32.046 --> 01:10:35.250
So where does this go
if we don't intervene?

01:10:35.250 --> 01:10:37.862
I think we know where it goes.

01:10:37.862 --> 01:10:39.360
You know, history tells us where it goes.

01:10:39.360 --> 01:10:43.920
The only thing is we probably
believe that we are immune

01:10:43.920 --> 01:10:46.098
because we are the
United States of America.

01:10:46.098 --> 01:10:47.910
And that stuff doesn't happen here.

01:10:47.910 --> 01:10:50.040
My only response to
that is that just shows

01:10:50.040 --> 01:10:51.340
you don't know our history

01:10:52.386 --> 01:10:53.340
cause that stuff has happened here.

01:10:53.340 --> 01:10:54.753
It was called redemption.

01:10:56.339 --> 01:10:57.390
This is real.

01:10:57.390 --> 01:11:01.920
So part of what the African
American Policy Forum has done

01:11:01.920 --> 01:11:06.573
is we've tried to take
a a page out of history.

01:11:07.462 --> 01:11:09.012
We've tried to create something

01:11:10.140 --> 01:11:12.330
that we call the Freedom Writers

01:11:12.330 --> 01:11:14.643
and Freedom Readers book tour.

01:11:14.643 --> 01:11:18.780
Our analogy was that Freedom Writers,

01:11:18.780 --> 01:11:21.234
these are the folks in the 60s

01:11:21.234 --> 01:11:22.860
who put their bodies on the line

01:11:22.860 --> 01:11:24.663
to fight against segregation.

01:11:25.890 --> 01:11:28.920
And their understanding of
what they were doing was,

01:11:28.920 --> 01:11:32.220
you know, power doesn't get
to tell us where to sit,

01:11:32.220 --> 01:11:37.220
where to eat, where to
study, where to pray.

01:11:37.639 --> 01:11:42.480
Just like power didn't tell
Freedom Writers where to go,

01:11:42.480 --> 01:11:44.100
where to be, how to vote.

01:11:44.100 --> 01:11:47.580
Today they don't get to tell
us what to read, what to write,

01:11:47.580 --> 01:11:49.353
what to learn, what to teach.

01:11:50.375 --> 01:11:52.440
And if we allow them
to tell us what to read

01:11:52.440 --> 01:11:56.160
and what to teach, they're
telling us who we can be.

01:11:56.160 --> 01:12:00.630
And that cannot be what
all of this movement

01:12:00.630 --> 01:12:03.540
for the last 60 years has been about.

01:12:03.540 --> 01:12:07.320
We cannot come to this point
where we're willing to pass on

01:12:07.320 --> 01:12:12.320
a legacy that is half full or
a baton that cannot be reached

01:12:12.390 --> 01:12:13.920
by the next generation.

01:12:13.920 --> 01:12:16.800
So we created a book tour.

01:12:16.800 --> 01:12:21.450
We took 19 of the titles
that have been banned

01:12:21.450 --> 01:12:26.310
in states around the country,
and we gave them away.

01:12:26.310 --> 01:12:28.710
We said, if they don't
want you to read it,

01:12:28.710 --> 01:12:30.450
you should take it as a reading list.

01:12:30.450 --> 01:12:32.580
And better yet, we're
gonna be your Amazon.

01:12:32.580 --> 01:12:33.933
We gonna bring it to you.

01:12:35.979 --> 01:12:37.500
Which is exactly what we've been doing.

01:12:37.500 --> 01:12:40.200
And we have found that when we are talking

01:12:40.200 --> 01:12:41.910
directly to people,

01:12:41.910 --> 01:12:44.850
we're not trying to mediate
through the media saying,

01:12:44.850 --> 01:12:48.030
well, actually, you know,
critical race theory

01:12:48.030 --> 01:12:51.480
is the legal infrastructure
behind structural racism.

01:12:51.480 --> 01:12:53.070
When we're not trying to talk to them,

01:12:53.070 --> 01:12:55.410
but we're actually talking
to people and saying

01:12:55.410 --> 01:12:57.060
the reason why your neighborhood

01:12:57.060 --> 01:12:59.010
is under sourced and underfunded

01:12:59.010 --> 01:13:01.200
is because of your zip code.

01:13:01.200 --> 01:13:03.252
It's because of the zoning.

01:13:03.252 --> 01:13:04.719
It's because of the tax base.

01:13:04.719 --> 01:13:06.270
It's because of the Federal Housing Act.

01:13:06.270 --> 01:13:08.640
We are making it clear and plain

01:13:08.640 --> 01:13:10.830
that what people wake up to every day

01:13:10.830 --> 01:13:13.800
is not based on what they've
done or their choices.

01:13:13.800 --> 01:13:17.670
It's based on what our
society has put into play

01:13:17.670 --> 01:13:20.115
and how it's structured for the future.

01:13:20.115 --> 01:13:23.400
When you tell people that
directly, they understand it,

01:13:23.400 --> 01:13:26.658
they support it, and they get mad

01:13:26.658 --> 01:13:29.019
that people are trying to
take it away from them.

01:13:29.019 --> 01:13:32.970
So what we're all about is
like I am all about go teach.

01:13:32.970 --> 01:13:36.060
I am about taking this
information to families.

01:13:36.060 --> 01:13:38.910
I am about political activation,

01:13:38.910 --> 01:13:42.720
but not just in the small d
way, not even the big D way,

01:13:42.720 --> 01:13:44.460
but in the way that says,

01:13:44.460 --> 01:13:47.490
if we do not fight for
our democracy right now,

01:13:47.490 --> 01:13:50.160
we will not have it to
fight for in the future.

01:13:50.160 --> 01:13:52.680
And that is what I think is at stake.

01:13:52.680 --> 01:13:55.860
It might look like it's just
about a little thing right now.

01:13:55.860 --> 01:13:58.560
It might look like it's just about AP.

01:13:58.560 --> 01:14:00.935
It might look like it's
about intersectionality,

01:14:00.935 --> 01:14:03.450
critical race theory,
queer theory, but trust,

01:14:03.450 --> 01:14:06.510
if they are successful in the red states

01:14:06.510 --> 01:14:09.538
and in some sections of the blue states

01:14:09.538 --> 01:14:10.590
in making this the law,

01:14:10.590 --> 01:14:13.996
it will be the future
that we will inherit.

01:14:13.996 --> 01:14:16.163
And I frankly don't think
that that's what we want.

01:14:17.079 --> 01:14:20.246
(audience applauding)

01:14:27.957 --> 01:14:29.542
- Thank you, Kim.

01:14:29.542 --> 01:14:32.381
So we have a couple of
minutes left, and I just want,

01:14:32.381 --> 01:14:34.135
so my understanding is the reception

01:14:34.135 --> 01:14:35.579
will have some of those books.

01:14:35.579 --> 01:14:37.158
We'll have materials about the campaigns,

01:14:37.158 --> 01:14:38.836
how people can get involved in them,

01:14:38.836 --> 01:14:41.790
a chance to talk to the staff
about those possibilities.

01:14:41.790 --> 01:14:45.517
A couple of questions here, and this,

01:14:45.517 --> 01:14:47.070
and I'm just gonna go one more.

01:14:47.070 --> 01:14:48.640
The first word from Daisha was

01:14:49.593 --> 01:14:50.987
"The College Board is not our friend."

01:14:53.356 --> 01:14:54.517
"And, you know, the defense of them,

01:14:54.517 --> 01:14:57.587
"or the surprise that their
speedy flip-flop demonstrates

01:14:57.587 --> 01:14:59.549
"a more significant issue at hand

01:14:59.549 --> 01:15:00.630
"as to what these attacks
were meant to cause."

01:15:00.630 --> 01:15:03.727
She actually says, and I
think this is really helpful,

01:15:03.727 --> 01:15:06.750
"They were meant to cause
us to be reactionary,"

01:15:06.750 --> 01:15:10.370
by which I think it also
means right to think about

01:15:10.370 --> 01:15:12.420
who among us we should be suspicious of,

01:15:12.420 --> 01:15:14.811
despise, et cetera, right?

01:15:14.811 --> 01:15:16.993
Whatever in anti solidarity.

01:15:16.993 --> 01:15:20.043
There's a couple of questions
that are actually focused on

01:15:20.043 --> 01:15:21.690
the very project of AP classes,

01:15:21.690 --> 01:15:25.380
which before this we know are
a part of a system of sorting,

01:15:25.380 --> 01:15:28.708
deeply inequitable in terms
of who has access to them,

01:15:28.708 --> 01:15:30.258
reproducing racial hierarchies.

01:15:31.314 --> 01:15:33.300
So how, I wanna just invite
you to all say a little bit,

01:15:33.300 --> 01:15:36.429
maybe just to start with
Rod, on the one hand,

01:15:36.429 --> 01:15:38.010
we don't wanna say this is insignificant

01:15:38.010 --> 01:15:39.870
because it's simply a, you know,

01:15:39.870 --> 01:15:42.120
kind of elite box of courses.

01:15:42.120 --> 01:15:43.950
But on the other hand, as you said,

01:15:43.950 --> 01:15:46.192
the only way to learn this

01:15:46.192 --> 01:15:47.340
is not simply through an AP class.

01:15:47.340 --> 01:15:50.345
So how do you think about that and yeah,

01:15:50.345 --> 01:15:52.590
the kind of the relationship
in the class on these broader,

01:15:52.590 --> 01:15:55.340
the broader histories and
genealogies of Black studies?

01:15:56.926 --> 01:15:59.747
- Yeah, I mean, I think that, you know,

01:15:59.747 --> 01:16:01.169
we are at a moment

01:16:01.169 --> 01:16:02.647
where this is not just
about the AP course, right?

01:16:02.647 --> 01:16:04.710
This is about the integrity of education,

01:16:04.710 --> 01:16:08.613
you know, at this
historical juncture, right?

01:16:11.009 --> 01:16:13.290
I mean, I will say that, you know,

01:16:14.445 --> 01:16:19.445
I didn't answer the emails from
the College Board last year

01:16:19.560 --> 01:16:22.600
and all the times that they
were sending me those emails

01:16:24.047 --> 01:16:25.523
because it's the College Board.

01:16:27.780 --> 01:16:30.180
I want the SAT to go away.

01:16:30.180 --> 01:16:33.683
You know? (audience applauding)

01:16:35.880 --> 01:16:39.569
I do not think that it is in keeping

01:16:39.569 --> 01:16:44.569
with the history of Black
progressive thought,

01:16:45.154 --> 01:16:46.410
which let's face it,

01:16:46.410 --> 01:16:48.423
that's the dominant of Black history.

01:16:49.490 --> 01:16:51.960
I mean, Black intellectual
history is Black progressives.

01:16:51.960 --> 01:16:53.269
You know, I mean,

01:16:53.269 --> 01:16:54.840
you can talk about like the
conservatives and whatever,

01:16:54.840 --> 01:16:57.692
but what are you gonna do
with the rest of those weeks?

01:16:57.692 --> 01:17:00.687
(audience laughing) You know?

01:17:00.687 --> 01:17:04.497
And so, you know, so I do not think

01:17:09.430 --> 01:17:13.912
that what we need to have is a more elite

01:17:13.912 --> 01:17:17.163
and now watered down
experience with Black studies.

01:17:18.352 --> 01:17:20.220
That is not in keeping with Black studies.

01:17:20.220 --> 01:17:23.882
CLR James said that Black studies

01:17:23.882 --> 01:17:25.882
is the critique of Western civilization.

01:17:27.890 --> 01:17:32.490
Okay, he didn't say it
was the purview of those

01:17:32.490 --> 01:17:33.843
with high GPAs.

01:17:35.309 --> 01:17:36.353
You know, he didn't say that.

01:17:37.393 --> 01:17:40.803
All right, so this is a
radically democratic project,

01:17:41.744 --> 01:17:44.733
and we need to preserve that
and insist upon that, okay?

01:17:46.803 --> 01:17:47.753
- Great, thank you, thank you.

01:17:49.365 --> 01:17:51.320
Maybe just a question for David,

01:17:51.320 --> 01:17:54.750
which is also about the
possibilities and imperatives

01:17:54.750 --> 01:17:57.670
to also transform the
way US history is taught

01:17:58.980 --> 01:18:03.980
and as an ongoing site of yeah struggle.

01:18:04.020 --> 01:18:07.048
- Well, first of all, US
history has transformed

01:18:07.048 --> 01:18:11.970
in enormous ways in the last decades.

01:18:11.970 --> 01:18:13.083
It simply has.

01:18:14.591 --> 01:18:16.113
Let's not kid ourselves.

01:18:17.582 --> 01:18:20.700
Now, does that always trickle
down to the high schools?

01:18:20.700 --> 01:18:21.543
Of course not.

01:18:22.668 --> 01:18:25.632
I've been teaching summer
institutes for secondary teachers

01:18:25.632 --> 01:18:28.250
for, I don't know, 27 years or something.

01:18:28.250 --> 01:18:29.500
I've taught thousands of,

01:18:30.720 --> 01:18:33.870
they're usually well
motivated high school teachers

01:18:33.870 --> 01:18:34.773
in the summer.

01:18:35.727 --> 01:18:37.170
They're hungry, they're eager.

01:18:37.170 --> 01:18:38.920
Often because they don't know much.

01:18:39.810 --> 01:18:42.202
They want to take a seminar on slavery

01:18:42.202 --> 01:18:44.003
because they didn't learn much about it.

01:18:45.104 --> 01:18:47.848
But that doesn't mean
they're not ready to learn

01:18:47.848 --> 01:18:50.548
and ready to be taught by
some of the best historians.

01:18:51.749 --> 01:18:53.005
So let's not kid ourselves.

01:18:53.005 --> 01:18:55.789
In fact, I wrote this piece
called "Trust the Teachers,"

01:18:55.789 --> 01:18:57.593
I guess in the Atlantic or someplace,

01:18:58.471 --> 01:18:59.880
and I said, look, you wanna blame somebody

01:18:59.880 --> 01:19:03.394
for all that's going on
about Black history and race

01:19:03.394 --> 01:19:04.290
all over curriculums.

01:19:04.290 --> 01:19:05.760
Blame us.

01:19:05.760 --> 01:19:06.930
Blame the historians.

01:19:06.930 --> 01:19:08.670
Bring it on.

01:19:08.670 --> 01:19:09.540
We'll take the fight.

01:19:09.540 --> 01:19:11.133
Don't blame the teachers.

01:19:12.240 --> 01:19:15.033
Trust the teachers to learn something.

01:19:15.900 --> 01:19:20.433
Last point, we all gotta start somewhere.

01:19:21.749 --> 01:19:23.160
Next fall, in late September,

01:19:23.160 --> 01:19:27.270
Gilda Lehrman Center and
maybe others here at Yale,

01:19:27.270 --> 01:19:29.190
are helping co-sponsor a conference

01:19:29.190 --> 01:19:32.440
at Flagler College in
St. Augustine in Florida

01:19:33.870 --> 01:19:36.420
in what, the leader there
of this effort calls

01:19:36.420 --> 01:19:37.893
behind the red curtain.

01:19:40.888 --> 01:19:42.688
Now that's a private college,

01:19:42.688 --> 01:19:46.287
although they are not
untied to state monies

01:19:46.287 --> 01:19:48.600
because most of their buildings
are like state landmarks.

01:19:48.600 --> 01:19:51.209
So they got some risk to take,

01:19:51.209 --> 01:19:53.793
and their president is on board, I think.

01:19:56.520 --> 01:19:59.230
But we're working with
some cool faculty there

01:20:00.169 --> 01:20:01.830
who wanna do like a two and a half day

01:20:01.830 --> 01:20:06.330
knockdown drag out conference
about how is history taught?

01:20:06.330 --> 01:20:07.740
How is Black history taught?

01:20:07.740 --> 01:20:08.940
How should it be taught?

01:20:08.940 --> 01:20:10.200
We're gonna bring in Kim.

01:20:10.200 --> 01:20:11.900
Kim doesn't know she's coming yet.

01:20:13.489 --> 01:20:15.510
We're gonna bring in Kim Crenshaw

01:20:15.510 --> 01:20:17.584
and a whole bunch of other people.

01:20:17.584 --> 01:20:19.860
We'll have keynotes, but teachers,

01:20:19.860 --> 01:20:23.100
Florida teachers with the guts to show up.

01:20:23.100 --> 01:20:25.052
And that's a question.

01:20:25.052 --> 01:20:26.100
We had some Florida teachers
here at our conference

01:20:26.100 --> 01:20:30.510
last fall who were frankly afraid to come.

01:20:30.510 --> 01:20:34.560
They can't even, they're
afraid to use their emails.

01:20:34.560 --> 01:20:35.910
Even in state universities.

01:20:35.910 --> 01:20:37.710
I have three former PhD students

01:20:37.710 --> 01:20:39.990
who teach in state
universities in Florida.

01:20:39.990 --> 01:20:42.810
I emailed them and said,
what are you all doing?

01:20:42.810 --> 01:20:45.109
Aren't you gonna surround
the capitol and raise hell?

01:20:45.109 --> 01:20:47.259
And they said, I'll
answer you on my Gmail.

01:20:49.208 --> 01:20:51.323
So this is kind of the
way they live every day.

01:20:52.470 --> 01:20:54.390
But let's not kid ourselves.

01:20:54.390 --> 01:20:57.090
But what happens in the academy,

01:20:57.090 --> 01:21:01.410
the history, we change all the
time and rewrite and rewrite

01:21:01.410 --> 01:21:03.360
and argue like hell with each other.

01:21:03.360 --> 01:21:05.550
And sometimes it gets into good textbooks.

01:21:05.550 --> 01:21:07.230
Usually it gets in good textbooks.

01:21:07.230 --> 01:21:08.970
Usually it gets out sometimes

01:21:08.970 --> 01:21:11.430
in synthetic works of narrative, we hope.

01:21:11.430 --> 01:21:13.470
And it gets out in biographies.

01:21:13.470 --> 01:21:18.470
But the task is to convert
these explosions of knowledge

01:21:18.592 --> 01:21:23.550
we have had in the academy
for two, three generations

01:21:23.550 --> 01:21:27.903
to the public schools, to
the public who wants to read.

01:21:29.123 --> 01:21:30.892
That's still our task.

01:21:30.892 --> 01:21:32.910
I don't have any perfect
formulas for doing that,

01:21:32.910 --> 01:21:34.560
but at least when you get tenure,

01:21:35.728 --> 01:21:38.728
get the hell out of your own
campus and talk to real people.

01:21:39.600 --> 01:21:42.230
Cause they actually do
wanna learn something.

01:21:42.230 --> 01:21:44.685
(audience applauding)

01:21:44.685 --> 01:21:46.133
And even before you get tenure.

01:21:48.813 --> 01:21:50.493
- Thank you, David.

01:21:53.486 --> 01:21:56.110
- But come down to St.
Augustine in late September.

01:21:56.110 --> 01:21:57.683
We're gonna raise hell
behind the red curtain.

01:21:59.476 --> 01:22:01.031
- We have, we're gonna do
two more quick questions.

01:22:01.031 --> 01:22:02.385
I have a short question for Jason,

01:22:02.385 --> 01:22:04.172
and then we're gonna turn
it to Kim to leave off.

01:22:04.172 --> 01:22:07.411
I should also say, obviously
where we are in New Haven,

01:22:07.411 --> 01:22:10.232
there's a public school
system with dynamic teachers,

01:22:10.232 --> 01:22:11.905
incredible students.

01:22:11.905 --> 01:22:14.340
That's been consistently,
consistently denied

01:22:14.340 --> 01:22:15.933
the resources it needs.

01:22:17.040 --> 01:22:18.255
- And a teacher shortage.

01:22:18.255 --> 01:22:20.940
- Yes, and a very powerful
and growing movement

01:22:20.940 --> 01:22:24.849
among teachers, parents, and
students to insist, you know,

01:22:24.849 --> 01:22:26.811
the same things that are being said there

01:22:26.811 --> 01:22:28.292
that exist in our own state,

01:22:28.292 --> 01:22:30.000
which has among the
deepest racial inequalities

01:22:30.000 --> 01:22:32.385
and economic disparities.

01:22:32.385 --> 01:22:33.990
So I hope everyone is also
on the lookout for that work

01:22:33.990 --> 01:22:36.633
cause there's concrete opportunities.

01:22:36.633 --> 01:22:38.550
And we know in this institution at Yale

01:22:38.550 --> 01:22:41.070
have consciousness that
many of those disparities

01:22:41.070 --> 01:22:43.712
are linked to the resources we have

01:22:43.712 --> 01:22:45.893
because of the taxes that
we don't pay into it.

01:22:47.108 --> 01:22:50.675
So there's a version of that, right,

01:22:50.675 --> 01:22:52.551
that we have to account for here as well.

01:22:52.551 --> 01:22:54.356
I wanna just, a quick question to Jason

01:22:54.356 --> 01:22:56.196
and then some final words from Kim.

01:22:56.196 --> 01:22:58.274
History is one side of
these contestations,

01:22:58.274 --> 01:23:00.132
but there's many other disciplines.

01:23:00.132 --> 01:23:01.514
Sometimes people think,

01:23:01.514 --> 01:23:03.107
let's say a discipline like classics.

01:23:03.107 --> 01:23:06.606
Well by its nature, right,
all the classics folks,

01:23:06.606 --> 01:23:07.615
you're excused.

01:23:07.615 --> 01:23:08.932
This doesn't involve you, right?

01:23:08.932 --> 01:23:10.380
But in fact, that's the very site

01:23:10.380 --> 01:23:12.210
where certain authoritative forms

01:23:12.210 --> 01:23:15.074
of racialized knowledge are constituted.

01:23:15.074 --> 01:23:16.949
So can you just say a
little bit more about those

01:23:16.949 --> 01:23:18.818
and then we'll turn to
Kim for (audio blurs)

01:23:18.818 --> 01:23:20.969
- Seeing that DeSantis
is going hard on this.

01:23:20.969 --> 01:23:23.417
He's saying, we're gonna
do classical education.

01:23:23.417 --> 01:23:25.927
We're gonna have (audio cuts out)

01:23:27.376 --> 01:23:28.209
We're gonna make sure
everyone reads just classics.

01:23:28.209 --> 01:23:30.690
And now this is something I learned

01:23:30.690 --> 01:23:33.655
from my friend Charles Mills who said,

01:23:33.655 --> 01:23:35.970
you know who's channeling Plato now?

01:23:35.970 --> 01:23:38.070
It's the Black intellectual tradition.

01:23:38.070 --> 01:23:40.652
Who's doing philosophy of education?

01:23:40.652 --> 01:23:42.030
In my philosophy of education class,

01:23:42.030 --> 01:23:45.010
I begin with Plato and
I end with Bell Hooks

01:23:45.990 --> 01:23:48.270
because in the 20th century,

01:23:48.270 --> 01:23:52.683
who is talking about education
in a political system?

01:23:53.597 --> 01:23:56.010
It is the people denied that education.

01:23:56.010 --> 01:23:58.410
Those are where the
conversations are happening.

01:23:59.522 --> 01:24:03.890
So if you're going to
teach classical education

01:24:03.890 --> 01:24:07.230
in the 20th century,
that's really gonna end,

01:24:07.230 --> 01:24:09.510
those questions, those set of questions.

01:24:09.510 --> 01:24:13.285
When Plato in the Republic
says, when he's asked,

01:24:13.285 --> 01:24:15.930
what's the first question
of justice in the city?

01:24:15.930 --> 01:24:18.618
What's the first issue?

01:24:18.618 --> 01:24:20.460
He says how you educate people.

01:24:20.460 --> 01:24:24.240
And who in the United States
writes about education?

01:24:24.240 --> 01:24:26.583
Who are the great education theorists?

01:24:27.460 --> 01:24:28.440
Well, it starts with an argument.

01:24:28.440 --> 01:24:33.440
I mean, it goes, you know,
Anna Julia Cooper, Dubois,

01:24:33.480 --> 01:24:37.926
all the way to Bell Hooks and beyond.

01:24:37.926 --> 01:24:38.999
That's a conversation.

01:24:38.999 --> 01:24:40.590
Those classical questions,

01:24:40.590 --> 01:24:43.380
those are the Black
intellectual traditions.

01:24:43.380 --> 01:24:46.953
So don't let them take the
classical tradition from you.

01:24:48.279 --> 01:24:49.373
- Great, thank you, yeah.

01:24:51.979 --> 01:24:53.281
All right.

01:24:53.281 --> 01:24:55.219
This has been amazing, Kim, right?

01:24:55.219 --> 01:24:58.173
It's really, really, and
obviously more to come.

01:25:00.275 --> 01:25:01.302
There is a question here,

01:25:01.302 --> 01:25:05.553
but I don't, that points out
DeSantis is a Yale graduate.

01:25:06.420 --> 01:25:08.463
I think David Coleman.

01:25:10.881 --> 01:25:12.100
- [David] It's our responsibility.

01:25:12.100 --> 01:25:15.641
- Yeah. (Panelists quietly talking)

01:25:15.641 --> 01:25:17.173
- Is that a defense?

01:25:17.173 --> 01:25:18.295
- [David] No!

01:25:18.295 --> 01:25:20.543
- It's more of a, like,
what's going on here?

01:25:23.619 --> 01:25:27.150
- But I think there's a
way that the question,

01:25:27.150 --> 01:25:30.535
you know, a cynicism
can take over us, right?

01:25:30.535 --> 01:25:31.980
We think like we're simply,

01:25:31.980 --> 01:25:34.350
like nothing can be
done other than to just,

01:25:34.350 --> 01:25:38.100
we're so deeply en entrenched
in the kind of belly of it.

01:25:38.100 --> 01:25:39.962
What's a role?

01:25:39.962 --> 01:25:42.540
And what part of what the
question made me think about

01:25:42.540 --> 01:25:44.555
was the legacy of critical race theory,

01:25:44.555 --> 01:25:48.043
which actually emerges
from similar institutions,

01:25:48.043 --> 01:25:53.043
but actually challenges,
contests and says,

01:25:53.099 --> 01:25:53.932
what you're doing is not,

01:25:53.932 --> 01:25:55.530
it's not just we want more from it.

01:25:55.530 --> 01:25:59.310
We have a far, far more expansive sense

01:25:59.310 --> 01:26:00.840
of what education is for

01:26:00.840 --> 01:26:04.110
than the parochial mediocre
education you're providing us.

01:26:04.110 --> 01:26:05.880
So could you just as we like,

01:26:05.880 --> 01:26:09.270
help us think maybe about
from these sites, right,

01:26:09.270 --> 01:26:11.100
what those lessons might teach us

01:26:11.100 --> 01:26:12.900
about the connections we can make.

01:26:12.900 --> 01:26:14.763
Is there a CRT lesson in here for us?

01:26:16.110 --> 01:26:17.133
- Okay, Danny.

01:26:18.240 --> 01:26:22.443
So you have me trying to tell
a story in two minutes, right?

01:26:22.443 --> 01:26:24.093
So here's what,

01:26:25.096 --> 01:26:27.060
where I'll start and hopefully finish

01:26:27.060 --> 01:26:29.583
with just a note on Tyre Nichols.

01:26:31.140 --> 01:26:34.216
You know, one way in which I think

01:26:34.216 --> 01:26:36.180
those of us who are concerned
about the trajectory

01:26:36.180 --> 01:26:38.550
of the attack on education
and those who are doing it.

01:26:38.550 --> 01:26:40.300
One thing that we agree on

01:26:41.617 --> 01:26:46.617
is that education has been
and continues to be the key

01:26:47.250 --> 01:26:49.350
to transforming the society.

01:26:49.350 --> 01:26:51.720
The only difference is
a normative difference.

01:26:51.720 --> 01:26:56.073
We believe in the transformation,
and they don't, right?

01:26:56.919 --> 01:27:00.780
But we converge on the
absolute critical dimension

01:27:00.780 --> 01:27:03.990
of having access to education

01:27:03.990 --> 01:27:06.960
and the production of
new knowledge, right?

01:27:06.960 --> 01:27:10.500
So I will say then in
about public education,

01:27:10.500 --> 01:27:12.600
I know you guys have your debate about it,

01:27:13.963 --> 01:27:16.935
but public education was
the product of freed people

01:27:16.935 --> 01:27:19.721
during reconstruction, right?

01:27:19.721 --> 01:27:21.030
The first thing they wanted to vote.

01:27:21.030 --> 01:27:23.670
The second thing they
wanted was education.

01:27:23.670 --> 01:27:26.196
It was the thing that,

01:27:26.196 --> 01:27:28.680
was the second thing that
redeemers went after.

01:27:28.680 --> 01:27:29.940
First they went after the vote,

01:27:29.940 --> 01:27:32.130
and then they burned
down the school houses.

01:27:32.130 --> 01:27:35.070
That relationship continues to this day.

01:27:35.070 --> 01:27:38.670
So the same states that
have these memory laws

01:27:38.670 --> 01:27:42.612
and the efforts to suppress education

01:27:42.612 --> 01:27:44.501
are the same ones that
are trying to suppress

01:27:44.501 --> 01:27:45.496
the right to vote.

01:27:45.496 --> 01:27:48.390
We need to learn about what
those connections tell us

01:27:48.390 --> 01:27:51.240
about the significance of institutions

01:27:51.240 --> 01:27:53.340
that produce the knowledge that we need

01:27:53.340 --> 01:27:56.397
to sustain our democracy, right?

01:27:56.397 --> 01:27:58.740
So these things have always
been one and the same.

01:27:58.740 --> 01:28:00.240
They've always been connected.

01:28:00.240 --> 01:28:03.720
All sides or both sides
of this agree to it.

01:28:03.720 --> 01:28:06.150
We realize that there's a connection

01:28:06.150 --> 01:28:07.740
between a past that said

01:28:07.740 --> 01:28:09.930
if you taught an enslaved person to read,

01:28:09.930 --> 01:28:11.430
you could be punished.

01:28:11.430 --> 01:28:12.960
And if you were that enslaved person,

01:28:12.960 --> 01:28:14.460
you could be punished with death.

01:28:14.460 --> 01:28:17.730
That tells us that a
status quo is maintained

01:28:17.730 --> 01:28:20.250
by denying people who
are at the bottom of it

01:28:20.250 --> 01:28:24.600
the capacity to read the
situation, to be literate about it,

01:28:24.600 --> 01:28:25.890
to be able to change it.

01:28:25.890 --> 01:28:29.370
So that's a continuing dynamic

01:28:29.370 --> 01:28:30.900
that we are still in the middle of,

01:28:30.900 --> 01:28:33.540
and we need to be able
to pull those lessons

01:28:33.540 --> 01:28:35.040
to the forward moment.

01:28:35.040 --> 01:28:38.370
Now the reality is that
we have made strides

01:28:38.370 --> 01:28:40.440
as, you know, David has said.

01:28:40.440 --> 01:28:43.020
These institutions do not look the same.

01:28:43.020 --> 01:28:48.020
This does not look like the
same I'm sure 30, 40 years ago,

01:28:48.090 --> 01:28:50.310
it wouldn't have looked like this, right?

01:28:50.310 --> 01:28:52.230
That is the product of pressure.

01:28:52.230 --> 01:28:54.134
That is the product

01:28:54.134 --> 01:28:56.397
of imagining the
university being different.

01:28:56.397 --> 01:28:59.823
That is the product of bringing
our political material,

01:29:00.690 --> 01:29:02.550
our political connections,

01:29:02.550 --> 01:29:05.880
all the things that make for
transformative possibilities

01:29:05.880 --> 01:29:07.259
to bear.

01:29:07.259 --> 01:29:08.550
That is what happened.

01:29:08.550 --> 01:29:11.850
Critical race theory is a product of that.

01:29:11.850 --> 01:29:15.780
It comes from ethnic
studies as applied to law.

01:29:15.780 --> 01:29:19.740
It comes from African American
studies as applied to law.

01:29:19.740 --> 01:29:22.740
If we realize that the
inequalities that we face

01:29:22.740 --> 01:29:24.919
are not the product of our own deficits,

01:29:24.919 --> 01:29:28.740
but deficits in our structures,
in our institutions,

01:29:28.740 --> 01:29:30.540
then the question is,

01:29:30.540 --> 01:29:33.210
well how do the different
disciplines that we are in

01:29:33.210 --> 01:29:34.503
contribute to that?

01:29:35.482 --> 01:29:38.400
And so we went into law
school asking that question.

01:29:38.400 --> 01:29:41.820
How does law contribute to naturalizing

01:29:41.820 --> 01:29:43.800
an unequal status quo?

01:29:43.800 --> 01:29:46.800
First by depriving individuals and groups

01:29:46.800 --> 01:29:48.450
of the ability to change that

01:29:48.450 --> 01:29:51.960
and then rationalizing
saying that it's colorblind,

01:29:51.960 --> 01:29:53.760
so it's okay.

01:29:53.760 --> 01:29:56.550
So effectively the story
of critical race theory

01:29:56.550 --> 01:29:59.910
is the story of the institution
of education written large.

01:29:59.910 --> 01:30:02.490
Young people coming
into these institutions

01:30:02.490 --> 01:30:04.260
with a different idea

01:30:04.260 --> 01:30:06.570
about what the purpose of education is,

01:30:06.570 --> 01:30:08.400
a different idea about what the mission

01:30:08.400 --> 01:30:12.630
of the institution is, and
a lower level of tolerance

01:30:12.630 --> 01:30:15.540
for the failure to get what
they think they deserve

01:30:15.540 --> 01:30:17.280
out of these institutions.

01:30:17.280 --> 01:30:19.860
So I wanna suggest that the story

01:30:19.860 --> 01:30:22.710
of African-American
studies and ethnic studies

01:30:22.710 --> 01:30:25.110
and queer studies and
critical race theory,

01:30:25.110 --> 01:30:28.830
that is the history that
we have to bring to bear.

01:30:28.830 --> 01:30:30.750
Those are the stories that tell us

01:30:30.750 --> 01:30:33.930
that we don't have to accept
the limited parameters

01:30:33.930 --> 01:30:35.700
of the education that we have.

01:30:35.700 --> 01:30:38.100
It tells us that when we coordinate,

01:30:38.100 --> 01:30:39.630
when we make our demands,

01:30:39.630 --> 01:30:41.760
and when we believe they're legitimate,

01:30:41.760 --> 01:30:43.620
and we come up with alternative ways

01:30:43.620 --> 01:30:46.110
of delivering knowledge and information,

01:30:46.110 --> 01:30:48.210
we actually change things.

01:30:48.210 --> 01:30:51.193
So now this is the pushback against it.

01:30:51.193 --> 01:30:54.270
With every transformative
moment there is a pushback,

01:30:54.270 --> 01:30:56.610
and they're just happening quicker.

01:30:56.610 --> 01:30:59.970
So 2020 transformative possibility,

01:30:59.970 --> 01:31:03.540
2022, 23, massive pushback.

01:31:03.540 --> 01:31:06.450
Once we understand that this
is all part of the dynamic,

01:31:06.450 --> 01:31:09.660
we don't have to accept this
is just the way things are.

01:31:09.660 --> 01:31:12.810
This is the way things
have been made to be.

01:31:12.810 --> 01:31:15.420
And we yes can do something about it

01:31:15.420 --> 01:31:17.163
because we already have.

01:31:18.302 --> 01:31:22.719
- Yeah, great. (audience applauding)

01:31:26.952 --> 01:31:28.309
I'm so grateful for this discussion.

